/* 雅思口语参考答案 —— 由 tools/生成答案.py 生成，不要手改 */
window.IELTS_ANSWERS = {"source":"雅思口语参考答案（2026 年 9-12 月题库）","p1":{"Travelling":{"q":["What transport do you often use when travelling?","Do you often ride a bicycle?","Do you prefer to sit by the window when travelling?","Did you have a long journey with your family as a child?"],"a":["Trains, mostly. If it's a short trip I'll take the high-speed rail, because it gets me there in a couple of hours and I can read on the way instead of sitting in traffic.","Not as often as I'd like. I cycle to the market at weekends, but the roads near my place aren't very bike-friendly, so I don't risk it during rush hour.","Definitely. I like watching the scenery go by, and on a long journey it gives me something to look at. An aisle seat just feels a bit exposed.","Yes, quite a few. Every summer we'd drive about six hours to visit my grandparents, and I still remember how endless those journeys felt. We played cards in the back seat to pass the time."]},"Rubbish and recycling":{"q":["Did you recycle when you were a kid?","Is waste sorting common in your country?","How do you recycle things like paper and plastic?","What do you do when you see rubbish on the street?"],"a":["Not really, no. Sorting rubbish wasn't a thing back then, and everything went into one bin. I only got into the habit a few years ago, when my neighbourhood started enforcing it.","It's becoming common, at least in big cities like Shanghai. Every residential compound has four colour-coded bins now, and there are usually volunteers standing by to check. In smaller towns it's much less strict.","I keep two boxes at home, one for paper and one for plastic bottles and packaging, and take them down when they're full. Batteries and old electronics I drop off at a separate collection point.","If it's something I dropped, I pick it up. If it's other people's, I usually just walk past, which I'm not proud of. I'd only really step in if it were somewhere children play."]},"Tiredness":{"q":["What do you do when you feel tired?","When would you feel tired?","Do you often feel tired?","When you feel tired, do you still go out with your friends?"],"a":["It depends how tired I am. If it's just the afternoon slump, I'll make a coffee and take a short walk. If I'm properly worn out, I lie down for twenty minutes, and that resets me better than caffeine.","Usually mid-afternoon, around three, and then again after a long stretch of teaching with no break. Late nights also catch up with me the next day.","Fairly often, yes. My schedule is quite packed, so by Thursday I'm running on empty. I've started going to bed earlier, which has helped a bit.","Sometimes, if it's something I've been looking forward to. I'll show up, but I'll leave early. If it's just a casual drink, I'd rather stay in and sleep."]},"Shoes":{"q":["Which do you prefer, fashionable shoes or comfortable shoes?","How much money do you usually spend on shoes?","Have you ever bought shoes online?","Do you like buying shoes? How often?"],"a":["Comfortable, without question. I'm on my feet most of the day, so if a pair isn't comfortable I won't wear it twice. I do like a good-looking trainer, but comfort comes first.","Not a huge amount, maybe five or six hundred yuan a pair, and I'll wear them for two or three years. I'd rather buy one decent pair than three cheap ones.","Yes, but it's a gamble. Sizes vary so much between brands that I've had to return a couple of pairs. Now I only buy online if I've tried the same model in a shop.","I don't mind it, but I don't enjoy it either. I probably buy a new pair twice a year, usually when the old ones start falling apart."]},"Politeness":{"q":["What did you do to show your politeness as a child?","On what occasions should we be polite?","Do you think being polite is very important?","How did you learn to be polite as a child?"],"a":["The usual things. I'd greet relatives when they came round, offer them tea, and never start eating before the adults. My parents were quite strict about saying hello properly.","Pretty much always, but especially with people who are serving you, like waiters, drivers and cleaners. And in someone else's home, where you're a guest and the rules aren't yours.","I do. It costs nothing and it makes everyday interactions much smoother. I'd say it matters more than being clever, honestly, because politeness opens doors that intelligence alone doesn't.","Mostly by copying my parents, and by being corrected a lot. If I was rude to a shop assistant, my mother would make me apologise on the spot, and that stuck."]},"Fruit and vegetables":{"q":["Do people in your country like planting vegetables?","Were there any kind of fruits and vegetables you disliked as a child?","What kind of fruits and vegetables do you dislike?","Where do you usually buy fruit and vegetables?","How often do you eat fruit and vegetables?"],"a":["Some do, especially older people. A lot of them grow tomatoes or spring onions on a balcony, and in the countryside almost everyone has a small plot. It's caught on among younger people too.","Bitter melon, definitely. My grandmother loved it and kept putting it on my plate. I also refused to eat carrots for years, which I've since got over.","Bitter melon is still not my thing, and I'm not keen on celery either, mostly because of the texture. Everything else I'm fairly easy about.","There's a wet market five minutes from me, so that's my default. The produce is fresher than in the supermarket and cheaper too. I order online occasionally when I'm short of time.","Every day. Fruit with breakfast, and vegetables with at least two meals. I try to keep it varied rather than eating the same three things all week."]},"Advertisement":{"q":["Do you often see advertisements when you are on your phone or computer?","What kind of advertising do you like?","Do you like advertisements?","Do you see a lot of advertising on trains or other transport?","Is there an advertisement that made an impression on you when you were a child?","Have you ever bought something because of an advertisement?"],"a":["Constantly. Every app opens with a splash ad, and short videos are half advertising now. I've got an ad blocker on my laptop, but on my phone I just put up with it.","I quite like ads that tell a story or make me laugh, the ones that are almost a short film. What I can't stand is the repetitive shouting kind.","Not really, but I accept them. They pay for the free services I use, so I can't complain too much. I just wish there were fewer of them.","Yes, a lot. The metro is covered in it, with posters in the carriages, screens above the doors, even on the ticket machines. On the high-speed rail they hand out leaflets.","There's an ad for a milk brand that I still remember the jingle of. It had a cartoon cow, and every child in my class could sing it. That's the power of a good tune, I suppose.","Yes, more than once. The most recent was a pair of running shoes I saw in a short video, because the ad made them look effortless. They were fine, but not life-changing."]},"Paper":{"q":["Do you carry paper and pens with you when you go out?","Do people still keep handwritten letters today?","Do you still write physical letters?","What did you like to do with paper as a child?","Have you made any crafts with paper?"],"a":["I usually have a small notebook and a pen in my bag. I write my lesson plans by hand first and then type them up, because typing straight away never works for me.","A few do, but it's rare. I've kept maybe a handful from my school days. Most people my age would see a handwritten letter as a real gift now, precisely because it's so unusual.","Rarely, maybe a card at New Year, and everything else goes by message. I do write notes by hand, though, and I think handwriting still carries more warmth.","I folded a lot of paper planes and made little paper boats to float in the sink. I also drew comics on any scrap paper I could find.","A few. I can fold a crane and a decent paper plane, but that's about my limit. My students are far better at it than I am."]},"Secondary school":{"q":["Is there anything you miss about your secondary school?","What was your favourite subject at secondary school?","Were there any subjects that you found difficult at secondary school?","Do you remember your first day at secondary school?"],"a":["I miss the routine, oddly enough, seeing the same people every day and knowing exactly where I stood. And the canteen noodles, which I still think about.","Maths, which is probably why I teach it now. I liked that there was always a definite answer. You either got it or you didn't, and nobody could argue with you.","Physics, definitely. I could handle the formulas but never really understood what was going on physically. And I was hopeless at drawing, so art was a nightmare.","Very clearly. I got lost looking for my classroom and ended up in the wrong building. A teacher took pity on me and walked me over, which was embarrassing at the time."]},"Name":{"q":["Do you often forget people's names?","How do you feel when people can't remember your name?","How do you remember people's names?","Is it easy for you to remember people's names?"],"a":["All the time, and it's awkward. I'll remember a face and everything we talked about, but the name just vanishes. I've learned to say \"remind me of your name\" rather than guess.","I don't mind at all, because I'm exactly the same. What bothers me slightly is when someone remembers the wrong name and uses it confidently.","I repeat it back when I'm introduced, and try to link it to something, like a rhyme, a job, or someone else with the same name. Writing it down helps most, though.","No, it's one of my weak spots. New classes are the worst, thirty names in one go and I'm lucky to keep five."]},"Opportunities":{"q":["Do you often get new opportunities?","Do you like taking on new opportunities?","Should people try as many different things as they can?","Did you have any opportunities to take part in new activities as a child?"],"a":["Reasonably often, yes, more than I used to. Teaching brings a lot of them, with new courses and schools asking me to take on classes. I say yes to maybe half.","I do, but I've become more selective. Early on I said yes to everything and ended up exhausted. Now I ask myself whether it actually leads anywhere before committing.","Up to a point. Trying things is how you find out what you're good at, but jumping around constantly means you never get good at any one thing. I'd explore widely when you're young, then narrow down.","Quite a few. My parents signed me up for swimming, calligraphy and chess, mostly without asking me. Chess is the only one that stuck."]},"Music":{"q":["Do you prefer sad or happy music?","Does happy music make you feel more excited?","Have you taken any music classes?","Do you listen to music while doing other things?"],"a":["Happy music, on the whole. I mostly listen while I'm commuting or marking papers, and I want something that keeps my energy up. Sad songs are lovely, but only in small doses.","It does, especially anything with a strong beat. I use it before a long day of classes, and ten minutes of something upbeat does more for me than coffee. Slow music has the opposite effect.","Only the compulsory ones at school, where we learned to read simple notation and sang in a choir. I never took it further, which I slightly regret now.","Almost always, while cooking, walking or marking. The only time I don't is when I'm reading something difficult, because I can't concentrate with lyrics in the background."]},"Teachers":{"q":["Do you have a favorite teacher?","Do you want to be a teacher in the future?","Do you have a teacher from your past that you still remember?","Are you still in touch with your primary school teachers?","In what way has your favourite teacher helped you?","Do you like your primary school teachers more than your high school teachers?"],"a":["Yes, my maths teacher in secondary school. He was strict but fair, and he never made anyone feel stupid for asking a basic question. I think I teach a bit like him now.","I already am one, funnily enough. I've been teaching maths for several years now. It was never the plan, but I found I enjoyed explaining things.","Several, but one stands out, my form teacher in primary school. She noticed I was struggling at home and quietly looked out for me. I didn't appreciate it at the time.","Only with one, and just through social media. We exchange messages at New Year. The others I'd struggle to even find.","Mainly by taking me seriously. He'd stay behind to go through a problem again if I didn't get it, and he told me I was better at maths than I thought. That changed how I saw myself.","Not more, but differently. Primary teachers were warmer, they looked after you. High school teachers were more like coaches, pushing you towards exams. I needed both."]},"Social media":{"q":["Have you ever posted anything on social media?","When did you start using social media?","Do you think you spend too much time on social media?","Do your friends use social media?","What do people often do on social media?"],"a":["Very little. I'll occasionally post a photo of my cat or something I cooked, but I'm not one of those people who documents everything. I mostly just scroll.","Fairly late, in my early twenties, because I was suspicious of it for a long time. Now I use it mainly for work, since parents and students expect to find me there.","Probably yes, though less than I used to. I deleted the apps from my phone for a month and was amazed how much time came back. Now I check in short bursts.","All of them, to different degrees. Some post daily, some only watch. We mostly use it to arrange things rather than to talk on it.","Scroll, mostly. They watch short videos, follow people they'll never meet, and compare their lives to edited versions of other people's. And a great deal of messaging."]},"Tidiness":{"q":["Do you like to keep things tidy?","Did you keep your room tidy as a child?","How do you keep your work or study space tidy?","Do you think that it is necessary to be tidy?","Do you think there are people who can always stay tidy?","What do you think of the people who are not very tidy?"],"a":["I do, though I'm not obsessive about it. My desk has to be clear before I can work, because clutter genuinely distracts me. But don't open my wardrobe.","Not at all. My mother used to threaten to throw everything out. I only became tidy when I got my own place and realised I couldn't find anything.","Everything has a place, and I put things back immediately instead of leaving a pile. I also clear the desk completely at the end of each day, because starting fresh matters to me.","Necessary is a strong word, but it helps. A tidy space saves time because you're not hunting for things, and it makes you calmer. Beyond a point, though, it becomes its own obsession.","There must be, but I've never met one. Most people I know are tidy in some areas and chaotic in others. A friend's kitchen is spotless while her car is a disaster zone.","I don't judge them. Some of the most creative people I know work in chaos. As long as it doesn't spill into shared spaces, it's nobody else's business."]},"Websites":{"q":["What kinds of websites do you often visit?","What is your favourite website?","Are there any changes to the websites you often visit?","What kinds of websites are popular in your country?","Do you prefer getting information from websites or books?","Would you like to have your own website?","What have you learned from websites that help with your life or studies?"],"a":["Mostly news and reference sites, a couple of news portals, and Wikipedia more than I'd like to admit. Plus a video site for anything I need to learn how to do.","Probably a video site, because I use it for everything from cooking to fixing things to teaching ideas. It's replaced a whole shelf of books for me.","Plenty. They've all become more video-heavy and more cluttered with recommendations. Reading a simple article now means scrolling past three pop-ups.","Shopping sites, short-video platforms and news portals. Anything that combines entertainment with buying seems to do well. Older people tend to prefer news and health sites.","Websites for anything current or practical, books for anything that needs depth. If I want to understand a subject properly, a book forces me to slow down in a way a webpage doesn't.","I've thought about it, a simple one for my teaching with materials, schedules and maybe a blog. The barrier isn't technical, it's finding the time to keep it updated.","Practical things, mostly, like how to fix a leaking tap, how to structure a lesson, how to cook something properly. The internet taught me most of what school didn't."]},"Watch":{"q":["Do you wear a watch?","Have you ever got a watch as a gift?","Why do some people wear expensive watches?","Do you think it is important to wear a watch? Why?"],"a":["I do, a simple mechanical one. I got out of the habit for years because my phone did the job, but I've come back to it, since checking your wrist is far less rude than pulling out a phone.","Yes, my parents gave me one when I finished school. It wasn't expensive, but I still wear it, and it's the only thing I'd be upset to lose.","Partly as a status symbol, and partly because they genuinely appreciate the engineering. In some professions a good watch is almost expected, like a suit.","Not important, but useful. It stops you checking your phone every five minutes, and in a classroom it's much less disruptive. It's a small thing, but it changes how you come across."]},"Shopping":{"q":["Do you like shopping?","How often do you go shopping?","Do you prefer online shopping or in-store shopping?","Have you ever returned anything you bought online?"],"a":["Not really. I know what I want, I buy it, and I leave. Wandering around shops for hours is my idea of a wasted afternoon. I do enjoy buying books and kitchen things, though.","For groceries, a couple of times a week, usually at the wet market. For clothes, maybe every few months, and almost always online.","Online for anything I already know I want, because it's faster and cheaper. In-store for things I need to feel or try, like shoes or a jacket.","Several times. The last one was a pair of shoes that looked nothing like the photo. Returning them was easy enough, but the whole process put me off for a while."]},"Cars":{"q":["Did you enjoy traveling by car when you were a kid?","What types of cars do you like?","Do you prefer to be a driver or a passenger?","What do you usually do when there is a traffic jam?","Do you think car colours are important?","Will you buy an expensive car in the future?"],"a":["I loved it, as long as it wasn't too long. Sitting in the back with snacks and a window to look out of felt like an adventure. After four hours it stopped being fun.","Practical ones, honestly. I like small hatchbacks that are easy to park, and I've got a soft spot for old estate cars. Sports cars look great but make no sense in this city.","Passenger, easily. I can read or sleep, and I don't have to worry about parking. Driving in heavy traffic drains me in a way that being driven never does.","Put on a podcast and accept my fate, because getting annoyed doesn't move the cars. If I know the jam is bad, I'll take a different route or just leave later.","A bit. Light colours are safer at night and stay cooler in summer, and a strange colour hurts resale value. Beyond that, I don't think anyone really cares.","Unlikely. I'd rather spend that money on travel or on my flat. A car gets scratched, dented and stuck in traffic regardless of what it cost."]},"Public gardens and parks":{"q":["Did you like going to parks as a child?","Do you still like going to parks now?","Would you like to see more parks in your city?","Are there any parks you want to go to in the future?","Would you prefer to play in a personal garden or public garden？","How are the parks today different from those you visited as a kid?","What do you like to do when visiting a park？","Would you like to play in a public garden or park？"],"a":["Yes, there was a small park near my grandparents' place with a slide and a pond, and we'd go most evenings. I remember feeding the fish more than anything else.","I do, but for different reasons. Now it's about walking or running rather than playing. It's the only place in the city where nobody expects anything from you.","Definitely. My district is dense and the nearest decent park is a metro stop away. Small pocket parks on street corners would make a huge difference.","There's a big botanical garden on the edge of the city that I've never visited. I keep meaning to go in autumn, when the leaves turn.","A personal one, if I had it, because you can leave things out, go barefoot, and there's nobody to tell you off. But a public garden is better for meeting people.","They're much better equipped, with proper running tracks, exercise machines and good lighting. The downside is they're also far more crowded and more managed.","Walk, mostly, or run a few laps. I'll sit on a bench with a coffee if the weather's good, and occasionally take a book and get through a chapter.","A public one, in practice, since I don't have a garden and half the pleasure is watching other people, kids chasing each other, old men playing chess. A private garden would be quieter but lonelier."]},"Science":{"q":["Do you like science?","When did you start to learn about science?","Which science subject is interesting to you?","What kinds of interesting things have you done with science?","Do you like watching science TV programs?","Do Chinese people often visit science museums?"],"a":["I do, though more as a spectator than a practitioner. I like understanding how things work, why the sky's blue, how a vaccine trains your body. The maths behind it I find harder.","Properly in secondary school, though I was curious much earlier. My father used to explain things like why ice floats, and I found that far more interesting than the textbook.","Biology, probably. Living things are messy and full of exceptions, which makes them more interesting than physics. The human body especially, it's absurdly complicated.","Nothing very advanced. I've grown crystals with students, made a simple electric circuit, and once built a bottle rocket that went much further than expected.","Yes, the well-made ones. I'll watch a documentary about the ocean or space if it's on. I'm less keen on the ones that dramatise everything with loud music.","Families with children do, especially during holidays, because most of them are free and make a cheap day out. Adults on their own, much less so."]},"Mirrors":{"q":["Do you like looking at yourself in the mirror? How often?","Have you ever bought mirrors?","Do you usually take a mirror with you？","Would you use mirrors to decorate your room?"],"a":["Not particularly. I check myself before going out, same as everyone, but I don't linger. Mirrors in lifts make me slightly uncomfortable, to be honest.","One, when I moved into my flat. It's a plain rectangular one by the door, mostly so I don't leave the house with my hair sticking up.","No, never. My phone screen does the job if I need to check something. Carrying a mirror feels like a habit from another era.","I would, if the room were small, since a big mirror makes a space feel twice the size. I'd avoid a wall of them, though, because it starts to feel like a gym."]},"Outer space and stars":{"q":["Have you ever learnt about outer space and stars?","Do you like science fiction movies? Why?","Do you want to know more about outer space?","Do you want to go into outer space in the future?"],"a":["A bit at school, the planets, the solar system, that sort of thing. Beyond that it's mostly from documentaries. I couldn't tell you much about astrophysics.","Some of them. I like the ones that use space to ask questions about people, like Interstellar, rather than pure action. The ones that are all explosions bore me.","I'd like to, in a casual way. I'd love to be able to look up and name what I'm seeing. But I'm not going to start reading textbooks about it.","Not really. The idea is exciting for about five minutes, then I think about the cost, the training and the fact that there's no way back. I'd settle for a very good telescope."]},"Singing":{"q":["Do you like singing? Why?","Have you ever learnt how to sing?","Who do you want to sing for?","Do you think singing can bring happiness to people?","Do you like listening to others singing?","Have you ever taken a singing class?"],"a":["I like it, but I'm not good at it. I sing in the car and in the shower, where nobody can judge me. It's the quickest way to lift my mood.","Only in the school choir, which taught me to stay in tune with other people rather than to sing well on my own. I've never had a proper lesson.","Nobody, honestly. Maybe my cat, who has no opinions. If I had to, I'd sing for a room of children, because they're a forgiving audience.","Absolutely. It's one of the few things that works on you physically, you breathe deeper and your shoulders drop. Even bad singing in a group makes people feel closer.","Yes, when they're good. Live singing especially, even slightly imperfect, because there's something in the room that a recording doesn't have.","No, never formally. I've thought about it, a few lessons just to learn to breathe properly, but I always find something more urgent to spend the money on."]},"Clothing":{"q":["What kind of clothes do you like to wear?","Do you prefer to wear comfortable and casual clothes or smart clothes?","Do you like wearing T-shirts?","Do you spend a lot of time choosing clothes?","Do you wear different styles of clothes on weekdays and weekends?","What colour clothes do you like?"],"a":["Simple, comfortable things, plain shirts, dark trousers, a jacket. I don't like logos or big prints. My students probably think I dress like an accountant.","Comfortable and casual, though I have to look slightly smart for teaching. I've found a middle ground, soft shirts that look formal but feel like pyjamas.","Yes, in summer, and at home all year. A good plain T-shirt is the most useful thing in a wardrobe. I own far too many of them.","Very little. I decide the night before, usually in ten seconds. The only time I think about it is when I have to attend something formal.","Definitely. Weekdays it's shirts and proper shoes, weekends it's a hoodie and trainers. The moment I get home on Friday I change, and it feels like taking off a costume.","Dark colours, mostly, navy, grey, black, and a lot of white shirts. They match everything and don't show stains. I have one red jumper that I wear when I'm feeling brave."]},"Jokes & Comedies":{"q":["Are you good at telling jokes?","Do your friends like to tell jokes?","Do you like to watch comedies?","Have you ever watched a live show?","Are comedy shows popular in your country?"],"a":["Terrible. I always laugh before the punchline, which ruins it. I'm much better at reacting to other people's jokes than telling them.","A couple of them do, and they're good at it. One friend can make any story funny just through how he tells it. I'm envious of that.","Yes, especially stand-up. I like that it's just one person and a microphone, with no effects to hide behind. Sitcoms I find a bit formulaic after a while.","A few. I saw a stand-up show last year in a small venue, and it was far better than watching it on a screen. The room laughing together is half the experience.","Very. Stand-up in particular has exploded over the last few years, and there are comedy clubs in most big cities now. Sketch shows are popular on TV too."]},"Headphones":{"q":["Do you use headphones?","What type of headphones do you use?","When would you use headphones?","In what conditions would you not use headphones?","Is wearing headphones comfortable?"],"a":["Constantly. I wear them on the metro, while marking, and when I need to concentrate. They're the only way to create privacy in a shared space.","Over-ear ones for work, and small wireless earbuds when I'm out. The big ones sound better, and the small ones are the only thing that fits in a pocket.","On public transport, in cafés, and at home when my neighbours are being loud. Basically any time I want to control what I'm hearing.","When I'm walking alone at night, because I want to hear what's around me. And when I'm with other people, since wearing them in company is just rude.","The over-ear ones are, until after a couple of hours they start to press. Earbuds are worse, my ears ache after an hour. It's a trade-off between sound and comfort."]},"Morning time":{"q":["Do you spend your mornings doing the same things on both weekends and weekdays? Why?","Are there any differences between what you do in the morning now and what you did in the past?","What did you do in the morning when you were little? Why?","What do you usually do in the morning?","Do you like getting up early in the morning?"],"a":["No, they're completely different. On weekdays I'm up at six thirty and out the door by seven. At weekends I sleep until nine and then have a proper breakfast, which is the whole point of a weekend.","Yes, I get up much earlier now. As a student I'd sleep until noon if I could. Teaching forced the change, and now I actually prefer the early start.","I'd watch cartoons before school while my mother made breakfast. It was the only time of day when nothing was expected of me, so I guarded it fiercely.","Coffee first, then twenty minutes of reading or news, then a shower. If I'm organised I'll run before breakfast. I need that quiet hour or the whole day feels off.","I do now. The city's quiet, my head's clear, and I get more done before nine than in the whole afternoon. I wasn't always like this, though."]},"Work or studies":{"q":["What subjects are you studying?","Do you like your subject?","Why did you choose to study that subject?","Do you think that your subject is popular in your country?","Do you have any plans for your studies in the next five years?","What are the benefits of being your age?","Do you want to change your major?","Do you prefer to study in the mornings or in the afternoons?","How much time do you spend on your studies each week?","Are you looking forward to working?","What technology do you use when you study?","What changes would you like to see in your school?","What work do you do?","Why did you choose to do that type of work (or that job)?","Do you like your job?","What requirements did you need to meet to get your current job?","Do you have any plans for your work in the next five years?","What do you think is the most important at the moment?","Do you want to change to another job?","Do you miss being a student?","What technology do you use at work?","Who helps you the most? And how?"],"a":["I studied maths at university, and in a way I'm still studying it, because I teach it and I'm constantly going back over the material. Explaining something forces you to understand it properly.","I do, or I wouldn't still be doing it. What I like is that it's unambiguous, there's a right answer and a reason for it. That suits the way my brain works.","Partly because I was good at it, and partly because it kept my options open, since you can go into teaching, finance or research with it. Not very romantic, but practical.","Reasonably. Maths is compulsory, so everyone studies it up to a point, but few choose it at university. It has a reputation for being hard rather than interesting.","Nothing formal. I'd like to take a few short courses, maybe statistics, or something on how students actually learn, but I don't see myself doing another degree.","You've got enough experience to know what you're doing, and still enough energy to do it. And people take you seriously without expecting you to have everything figured out.","No, not now. It's too late to be useful, and I like where it's taken me. If I studied anything else it would be for pleasure, not for a career.","Mornings, definitely. My concentration peaks before eleven and collapses after lunch. Anything difficult gets done early or not at all.","These days it's more preparation than study, maybe ten hours a week of going over material and planning. When I was a student it was closer to thirty.","I'm already working, and I'd say yes, because work is more satisfying than studying in some ways. You see the results of what you do, which essays never really give you.","A tablet for notes, and a couple of apps for practice questions. I still use paper for anything I need to think through properly.","Smaller classes, above everything else. And less paperwork, because teachers spend far too much time filling in forms instead of teaching.","I teach maths, mostly to secondary school students, and a lot of it one to one. I also run a few small classes.","It started as a part-time job while I was studying, and I found I was good at it. Explaining something until someone's face changes is genuinely satisfying.","Mostly yes. The teaching I love and the admin I could live without. And the hours are better than people assume.","A degree in the subject, a teaching qualification and a background check, which is standard for anything involving children. Beyond that it was an interview and a trial lesson.","I'd like to build up my own classes rather than working for someone else. Nothing dramatic, just more control over what and how I teach.","Getting better at the job, honestly. I'm at the stage where a bit more skill makes a big difference, both to the students and to how tired I am at the end of the day.","Not at the moment. I've thought about it during bad weeks, but the thing I'd miss is the actual teaching, and most other jobs don't have that.","Sometimes, the freedom, and having an excuse to spend all day learning. I don't miss being broke or having exams.","A tablet instead of a whiteboard for a lot of it, which lets me save and share what I've written. And a messaging app for scheduling with parents.","A colleague who's been teaching for twenty years. She's seen every problem before, and five minutes with her saves me an hour of guessing."]},"Home/accommodation":{"q":["What kind of house or apartment do you want to live in in the future?","Are the transport facilities to your home very good?","Do you prefer living in a house or an apartment?","Please describe the room you live in.","What part of your home do you like the most?","How long have you lived there?","Do you plan to live there for a long time?","What’s the difference between where you are living now and where you have lived in the past?","Can you describe the place where you live?","What room does your family spend most of the time in?","What's your favorite room in your apartment or house？","What makes you feel pleasant in your home？","Do you think it is important to live in a comfortable environment？","Do you live in an apartment or a house?","Who do you live with?","What do you usually do in your apartment?","What kinds of accommodation do you live in?"],"a":["Something with a balcony and plenty of light, ideally on a lower floor. I'd like a small study with a door I can close, because right now my desk is in the corner of the living room and it's hard to switch off.","Very good, actually. There's a metro station about eight minutes' walk away and several bus routes. It's the main reason I haven't moved, even though the flat is small.","An apartment, for practical reasons, since there's less to maintain, better security, and I don't need a garden. If I had children I might feel differently.","It's small but well organised. A bed by the window, a desk against one wall, and bookshelves that have overflowed onto the floor. Not much else fits.","The desk by the window. It gets the morning sun and I do most of my work there. It's the only corner of the flat that's properly mine.","About four years now, long enough that I've stopped noticing the things I meant to fix.","For the foreseeable future, yes. The location is too good to give up and moving costs a fortune. I'd rather improve this place than start again somewhere else.","Quite a lot. My last place was older and darker, with a shared bathroom down the corridor. This one has its own kitchen, which sounds trivial until you've lived without it.","It's a one-bedroom flat on the fourth floor of an old building, recently renovated. Small, but the ceilings are high and there's more light than you'd expect.","The living room, without question. It doubles as a dining room and my workspace, so everything happens there. The bedroom is really just for sleeping.","The living room, for the same reason, since it's where the light is and where everything happens. In winter it's also the warmest room.","The morning light coming through the window onto the desk, and the fact that it's quiet. I can hear birds, which you don't expect in the middle of a city.","I think it matters more than people admit. A bad environment wears you down slowly, and you don't notice it, you just feel tired all the time. Fixing the light and the noise made more difference than anything else I did.","An apartment. Mine's small, but it's mine, and I like not having stairs to deal with.","On my own, with a cat who thinks she owns the place. It's quiet, which suits me.","Work, mostly. I mark papers at the desk and prepare lessons there. I cook properly at weekends. And I watch far too much on the sofa.","A rented one-bedroom flat in an old residential building. Nothing special from the outside, but the inside has been done up nicely."]},"Hometown":{"q":["Where is your hometown?","Is that a big city or a small place?","Please describe your hometown a little.","How long have you been living there?","Do you think you will continue living there for a long time?","Do you like your hometown?","Do you like living there?","What do you like (most) about your hometown?","Is there anything you dislike about it?","What's your hometown famous for？","Did you learn about the history of your hometown at school？","Are there many young people in your hometown?","Is your hometown a good place for young people to pursue their careers?","Have you learned anything about the history of your hometown?","Did you learn about the culture of your hometown in your childhood?"],"a":["Shanghai. I was born here and I've never really left. It's a place people either love or find exhausting, and I'm in the first camp.","Very much a big city, with twenty-odd million people, and it still feels like it's growing. There's no such thing as a quiet street in the centre.","It's a port city on the east coast, split by a river. The west side is older, with plane trees and nineteen-twenties buildings, and the east side is all glass towers. Most of the interesting food is in the old parts.","My whole life, apart from a few years away at university. I keep coming back, which probably says something.","I expect so. My work, my friends and my family are all here. The cost of living is the only thing that might push me out.","I do, despite its faults. It's efficient, it's safe, and there's always something open at two in the morning. The pace can be tiring, though.","On the whole, yes. The transport is excellent and you can get anything you need within ten minutes. What I don't like is how crowded everything is at weekends.","The convenience, probably. Everything works, the metro, the deliveries, the payments. When I travel I'm always struck by how much slower other cities feel.","The humidity in summer, and the cost of housing. Both are enough to make you consider leaving for a week or two every year.","Its skyline, its food, and its business district. The riverfront is what appears on postcards, but locals care more about the soup dumplings.","A little. We covered the colonial period and the war years, but in a fairly shallow way. Most of what I know now I picked up from museums and books.","A huge number. It's one of the main destinations for graduates from all over the country, which is part of why it feels so competitive.","For certain careers, definitely, finance, tech, design. There are more opportunities here than anywhere else in the country. The trade-off is the rent and the hours.","Enough to be interesting at dinner. I know about the foreign concessions, the nineteen-thirties boom and how the city rebuilt afterwards. I'd like to know more about the ordinary side of it, not just the famous buildings.","Mostly through food and festivals. My grandmother took me to the temple fair every year and we'd eat the seasonal things, mooncakes, dumplings, that sort of thing. That was culture to me, more than any lesson."]},"The area you live in":{"q":["Do you like the area that you live in?","Where do you like to go in that area?","Do you know any famous people in your area?","What are some changes in the area recently?","Do you know any of your neighbors?","Are the people in your neighborhood nice and friendly?","Do you live in a noisy or a quiet area?"],"a":["I do, mostly. It's an old residential district with a wet market, a couple of small parks and far too many noodle shops. It isn't fashionable, which is exactly why I can afford it.","The wet market in the mornings, and a small park about ten minutes away. There's also a tiny coffee place I go to when I need to work somewhere other than home.","None. It's a very ordinary neighbourhood, teachers, shopkeepers, retired people. The closest thing to a celebrity is the man who's run the same barber shop for thirty years.","Several. Two old buildings were knocked down and replaced with flats nobody local can afford, and a new metro line opened, which pushed the rents up. The market's still there, thankfully.","A few. I know the family opposite by sight and the woman downstairs by the noise her dog makes. We nod and say hello, but we're not close.","Reasonably. It's a city neighbourhood, so people keep to themselves, but they'll help if you need it. When my building flooded, three neighbours turned up with buckets.","Both, depending on the hour. It's quiet during the day, then at six the square fills up with people dancing to loud music. I've got used to it, but it took a year."]},"The city you live in":{"q":["What city do you live in?","Do you like this city? Why?","How long have you lived in this city?","Are there big changes in this city?","Is this city your permanent residence?","Are there people of different ages living in this city?","Are the people friendly in the city?","Is the city friendly to children and old people?","Do you often see your neighbors?","What's the weather like where you live?","Would you recommend your city to others?"],"a":["Shanghai. I've been here most of my life, and I teach here too.","I do. It's efficient in a way few cities are, with transport, deliveries and payments that just work. And there's a real mixture of old and new that I find interesting.","Essentially my whole life, minus a few years at university elsewhere. I came back as soon as I finished.","Constant ones. New metro lines, whole neighbourhoods rebuilt, and a skyline that changes every couple of years. It's exciting, but it does mean nothing stays familiar for long.","For now, yes. I rent rather than own, so permanent is a strong word, but I don't have plans to leave.","Very much so. There are students everywhere, young professionals in the centre, and plenty of older people in the older districts. It's more mixed than people assume.","Friendly but busy. Nobody will chat to you on the metro, but ask for directions and three people will help. It's a different kind of friendliness from a small town.","Better for old people than children, I'd say. There are parks, cheap transport and senior discounts. For children the schools are good, but space to play is scarce.","Fairly often, in passing, in the lift or at the market. We know each other's faces but not much more.","Four clear seasons, which I like. The summer is hot and humid with a rainy season in June, and winter is cold and damp without much snow. Spring and autumn are short but lovely.","To visit, absolutely, since the food and the riverfront are worth the trip. To live in, only if you can handle the pace and the rent."]},"Lost and found":{"q":["Have you ever lost your keys?","Will you post on social media if you lose your item?","Have you ever lost things?","Do you report to the police when finding something lost by others? Why?","What will you do if you find something lost by others?"],"a":["Twice, both times in the same coat pocket. The first time I panicked, and the second time I just retraced my steps and found them in a café. Now I keep a spare with a neighbour.","Probably, if it were something valuable. It's surprisingly effective in a neighbourhood where everyone's in the same group chats. For a cheap umbrella I wouldn't bother.","Constantly, umbrellas mostly, and a phone once on a bus. I've become quite philosophical about it, though losing a phone taught me to back things up.","If it's something valuable like a wallet or a phone, yes. It's the only way the owner has a chance, and it protects you from any suspicion. For a glove, no.","Look for an ID or a phone number first, then hand it to the nearest shop or building reception, or to the driver if it's on public transport. I've been on the other side often enough."]},"Computers/tablets":{"q":["Would you like to use a computer or tablet?","In what conditions would you use a computer or tablet?","When was the first time you used a computer or tablet?","What would your life be like without computers or tablets?","In what conditions would it be difficult for you to use a computer or tablet?"],"a":["I use both daily, a tablet for reading and marking, and a laptop for anything serious. I wouldn't want to rely on only one.","For work, and for anything that needs a screen bigger than a phone, forms, spreadsheets, long documents. Also for watching things in bed, which is a bad habit.","A desktop in a school computer room, probably around the age of ten. We were taught to type and to draw in Paint, and I remember thinking it was magic.","Slower and more paper-based. I'd write everything by hand and go to libraries to look things up. Honestly, parts of it sound quite appealing.","When my eyes are tired, or when I'm outside in bright sunlight, because the screen becomes unreadable. And on a crowded train if I haven't got a seat, it's hopeless."]},"Collecting things":{"q":["Do you collect things?","Where do you usually keep things you need?","Would you keep old things for a long time? Why?","Are there any things you have kept from your childhood?"],"a":["Not seriously. I keep ticket stubs from trips and a few old coins, but that's more sentiment than collecting. My cat collects hair ties, which is a more committed hobby.","In a drawer by the door for keys and wallet, and on the desk for anything work-related. I have a strict system that I follow about half the time.","Some. Old letters, a few books from school, things that belonged to my grandparents. They're not worth anything, but throwing them away would feel like throwing away the memory.","A few, a chess set, a box of letters and a terrible drawing I did of my family. My mother kept it and gave it back to me, which was oddly moving."]},"Street market":{"q":["Are there many street markets in China?","When was the last time you went to a street market?","Do you prefer to go shopping in the shopping mall or on the street market?","What do people usually buy on the street market?"],"a":["Lots, and they're coming back after years of being cleared away. Every neighbourhood has some version, vegetables, breakfast stalls, cheap clothes. Bigger cities have night markets too.","Last weekend, at the wet market near me. I go two or three times a week for vegetables, and it's the cheapest, freshest food in the city.","The street market, for food and everyday things, because it's cheaper and the produce is better. For anything with a guarantee, like electronics or shoes, I'd go to a mall or online.","Vegetables, fruit, meat and fish, mostly, plus breakfast, steamed buns, pancakes and soy milk. And cheap household things, socks, plastic boxes, phone cases."]},"Feeling bored":{"q":["Do you think childhood is boring or adulthood is boring?","What do you do when you feel bored?","When would you feel bored?","Do you often feel bored?"],"a":["Adulthood, easily. Children complain about being bored but they're usually just between games. Adults are bored for hours at a time, in meetings or on commutes, and can't do anything about it.","I go for a walk or call someone. Boredom usually means I've been staring at a screen for too long, and ten minutes outside fixes it more reliably than anything else.","In queues, in long meetings, and on journeys with no book. Also in the middle of the afternoon when I've finished everything and haven't started the next thing.","Rarely, to be honest. My work keeps me busy and I've always got something to read. The boredom I get is more like restlessness, wanting to be doing something else."]},"Friends":{"q":["Would you invite friends to your home?","Do you prefer to spend time with one friend or with a group of friends?","Do you have a friend you have known for a long time?","What do you usually do with your friends?","Where do you often meet each other?","Do you often go out with your friends?","How important are friends to you?","Why are some places suitable for meeting while others are not?","Is there a difference between where you meet friends now and where you used to meet them in the past?"],"a":["I do, though not as often as I'd like. My flat is small, so I'll have two or three people over for dinner rather than a crowd. Cooking for people is easier than going out, honestly.","One or two, definitely. In a big group you can't have a real conversation and you end up shouting over music. I'd rather have one proper talk than twelve shallow ones.","Yes, a couple from school. We don't see each other often, but when we do it's as if no time has passed. That kind of friendship doesn't need maintenance.","Eat, mostly. We'll go for noodles or cook at someone's flat, then walk somewhere. Occasionally a film, but mostly we talk.","At someone's home, or a small restaurant we all know. Nowhere too loud, because the whole point is talking.","Not as often as I used to. Everybody's busy now, so it's maybe once a fortnight, planned weeks in advance.","Very. They're the people you can be honest with, which you can't be at work. And they're the ones who notice when you're not yourself.","Noise, and whether you feel you can stay. A good place is quiet enough to talk and relaxed enough that nobody hurries you out. That's why cafés and kitchens work better than bars.","Yes, quite a difference. As a student it was cheap places near campus and we'd stay out late, whereas now it's someone's flat, earlier, and everyone's home by eleven."]}},"p2":{"对结果开心的重要决定":{"mat":"M2 教书十年","text":"Right, so the decision I've got in mind is leaving a school where I had a steady job in order to teach on my own, and it's probably the best thing I've done. I'd been teaching maths at a private school for about four years, and honestly I was comfortable. But I'd started to feel that the parts I actually enjoyed, sitting with one student and working through a problem until it clicked, were the parts I had least time for. Most of my day was meetings and paperwork. So I decided to go freelance and take on my own students. It wasn't an easy decision at all. I'm not a risk-taker by nature, and my parents thought I was mad. I gave myself a year and kept enough savings to survive six months without any income. What made me happy afterwards wasn't the money, it was the control. I could teach the way I thought was right, and I could see the difference in individual students. Within a year I had more work than I could take, which I really hadn't expected.","words":184,"p3":[{"q":"Which is better, to make a decision quickly or slowly?","a":"It depends on what's at stake. For small things, quickly is better, because deliberating over which coffee to order is just a way of wasting time. For anything that's hard to undo, slow is better, and I'd rather be seen as indecisive than get it wrong."},{"q":"Do you think most people's decisions are made in their 20s?","a":"A lot of the big ones are, yes, career, where to live, sometimes who to marry. But I don't think that's because people are wiser then. It's because that's when the system forces you to choose."},{"q":"What if people in a group have different opinions on a decision?","a":"Someone has to be given the final call, or the group will argue forever. In my experience the useful thing is to separate the decision from the discussion, let everyone talk, then let one person decide and take responsibility."},{"q":"What little decisions do people make every day?","a":"What to eat, what to wear, when to leave, whether to answer a message. Hundreds of them, and most are made without thinking at all."},{"q":"Do you think it is a good idea to make a small decision every day?","a":"I don't think it's necessary as a ritual, but there's something in it. Making one small deliberate choice a day, about what you eat or what you read, makes you notice how much of your day is automatic."},{"q":"How can we tell the difference between an important and an easy decision?","a":"By whether it's reversible. Easy decisions can be undone cheaply, so you can make them fast. Important ones close doors, and that's usually the test, not how much the decision costs."}]},"做困难事情并成功的人":{"mat":"M2 学生小周","text":"The person I want to talk about is a former student of mine, a boy called Zhou, who did something that I still think about when I'm having a bad day. When he first came to me he was in his second year of secondary school and getting around forty out of a hundred and fifty in maths. He wasn't lazy, he just had gaps going back years, and he'd convinced himself he was simply bad at the subject. That belief was the real problem. What he did was unglamorous. He came twice a week for eighteen months, and every session he brought a list of things he didn't understand, which takes real courage at that age. He redid exercises from two years earlier without complaining once. He ended up scoring over a hundred and ten in his final exam and getting into the school he wanted. Why he succeeded, I think, is that he stopped treating difficulty as evidence that he couldn't do it. I admire him more than most adults I know. He was fifteen, and he out-worked his own self-doubt.","words":185,"p3":[{"q":"In which sectors are companies more likely to succeed in China?","a":"Consumer technology, manufacturing and anything to do with delivery, I'd say. There's a huge domestic market and a supply chain that can produce almost anything quickly."},{"q":"Is it easy to start up a company?","a":"Starting is easier than it's ever been, because registration is quick and you can sell online on day one. Surviving the second year is the hard part, and most companies don't."},{"q":"Other than making money, what is more important for a company?","a":"Treating people decently, both staff and customers. A company that only optimises for profit tends to lose the good people first, and then the customers."},{"q":"Does money equal success?","a":"No, but it's a reasonable proxy for a company and a poor one for a person. I know people who earn a lot and are miserable, and teachers who earn little and are not."},{"q":"How can companies help protect the environment?","a":"By changing their defaults, not by asking customers to be virtuous. If the packaging is recyclable by default, nobody has to choose. Companies also have the scale to make recycling actually work."},{"q":"Is it difficult to do business in today's world?","a":"Harder than it looks from outside. Competition is global, customers are impatient, and the rules keep changing. The advantage is that the tools are cheap, so a small business can look big."}]},"快乐人士":{"mat":"M5 爷爷","text":"The happy person I'd like to describe is my grandfather, who's in his eighties now and still the most cheerful person in our family. He's a small man with very little hair and a permanent smile. He was a carpenter most of his life, and he still potters about with tools in the yard even though his hands aren't what they were. He talks to everyone, the vegetable seller, the neighbours, complete strangers at the bus stop, and he remembers all their names. How he shows happiness is quite quiet. He hums. He'll be sitting there repairing a stool and humming some old tune, and that's how you know he's content. He also cooks for people, which is his way of saying he likes you. I think he's happy because he doesn't compare himself to anyone. He never had much money and never seemed to want it. His pleasures are small and reliable, a good meal, a football match, a phone call from his grandchildren. When I'm stressed I think about him humming. It's a useful thing to have in your head.","words":182,"p3":[{"q":"When do you think people are happiest?","a":"Probably in ordinary moments rather than big ones, a meal with people they like, a free afternoon. Big events are exciting but they're over quickly."},{"q":"How can the arts spread positive emotions in society?","a":"By giving people something to share. A song or a film can make a whole room feel the same thing at once, and that's rarer than it sounds."},{"q":"Do you think teachers are able to spot the unhappy children?","a":"Often, yes, because they see them five days a week. But it depends on class size. In a class of thirty it's easy to miss the quiet one who never causes trouble."},{"q":"Should teachers know what children are doing all the time?","a":"No. That's surveillance rather than care. Teachers should notice changes, but children need some space that isn't watched."},{"q":"What would make a student happy at school?","a":"Being good at something, and being seen to be good at it. Also having one adult there who they feel is on their side."},{"q":"Do you think doing a job that you like is more important than other things?","a":"It's high on the list, but I wouldn't put it above health or people you love. A job you like is worth a lot, but it can't compensate for the other two being wrong."}]},"看过的名人采访":{"mat":"独立素材 · 作家访谈","text":"Not long ago I watched an interview with the Chinese writer Yu Hua, and it stuck with me much more than I expected. It was on a video platform, maybe forty minutes long, and it was part of a series where writers talk about their own books. He was mainly talking about how he came to write his best-known novel, and about the years before that, when he was a dentist in a small town and had no idea what he was doing. I watched it on my phone, late at night, which is usually a bad idea because I fall asleep. Not this time. What made it interesting was how funny he was. You'd expect a serious literary conversation, and instead he was telling stories about rejection letters and being sent home from the post office. It changed how I read his books, honestly. Once you've heard someone's voice, the writing sounds different. I felt oddly encouraged by it too, because he was a late starter who didn't seem to take himself too seriously. That's rarer than it should be.","words":182,"p3":[{"q":"What should people wear to a job interview?","a":"Something slightly smarter than the job requires, and nothing that distracts. The point is to look like you've made an effort, not to look fashionable."},{"q":"Why do we need to attend interviews when applying for jobs?","a":"Because a CV can't tell you whether someone can hold a conversation. Interviews are a poor test, but they're better than nothing at predicting how someone behaves."},{"q":"What should people do to prepare for a job interview?","a":"Find out what the organisation actually does, and prepare two or three concrete stories about things they've done. Most candidates can't do either."},{"q":"Why do some people think interviews are a waste of time?","a":"Because they reward confidence rather than competence. Someone who interviews well isn't necessarily good at the job, and plenty of good people freeze."},{"q":"Why do some people dislike being interviewed?","a":"It feels like being judged on twenty minutes, and the questions are often ones you can't prepare for. Also, most people dislike talking about themselves to a stranger."},{"q":"What kinds of people are usually interviewed?","a":"Anyone in the public eye, politicians, actors, athletes, and increasingly ordinary people who've done something unusual. The interview has become the standard format for finding out about someone."}]},"有条理的人":{"mat":"M9 朋友小林","text":"The organised person I know best is a friend of mine, Lin, who works as a graphic designer and runs her life like a small company. She plans everything. She has a paper diary, not an app, an actual diary, and everything goes in it in pencil, colour-coded. On Sunday evenings she plans the week, meals, deadlines, when she'll go to the gym. She also has a rule that she never leaves a room without putting one thing away, which sounds obsessive but works. I notice it most when we travel together. She'll have the tickets, the addresses, a backup plan and a rough idea of what everything costs, while I'm still looking for my passport. And she does it without being annoying about it, which is a skill in itself. I think being organised matters to her because her work is chaotic. Clients change their minds constantly, so the rest of her life has to be predictable or she'd fall apart. I've tried to copy about ten per cent of it. Even that has made a difference.","words":179,"p3":[{"q":"Do you think it is important for managers to be well organized?","a":"Very. If a manager isn't organised, the whole team spends its time waiting for information or redoing work. It's one of the few qualities that affects everyone else directly."},{"q":"Should parents teach their children to be organized?","a":"Yes, but by example more than instruction. Telling a child to tidy up while your own desk is a disaster doesn't work."},{"q":"What can people do to become more organized?","a":"Start with one system and keep it boring, a calendar, a list, a place for keys. Most people fail because they try to reorganise everything at once."},{"q":"Are organized people more likely to be successful at work or in their studies?","a":"At work, I'd say, because work involves other people and deadlines you don't control. Studying can be done in a panic at the last minute, and often is."},{"q":"Do you think children should learn to be organized from a young age?","a":"Some basic version, yes, like putting things away and knowing where their school things are. Nothing rigid, because small children need mess as well."},{"q":"Why is it important for people to be organized?","a":"Because it reduces the number of decisions you have to make. If everything has a place, you stop wasting attention on where things are."}]},"嘈杂地":{"mat":"M6 上海火车站","text":"One of the noisiest places I've ever been is the main railway station in Shanghai, on the first day of the Spring Festival travel rush. I was there about two years ago, at six in the morning, seeing a friend off. The hall must have held a couple of thousand people, all with suitcases and boxes, and every single one of them seemed to be shouting into a phone at the same time. What made it noisy wasn't any single sound, it was the layering. Announcements every thirty seconds, trolleys rattling, a child screaming somewhere, and this constant low roar of conversation that you couldn't pick apart. Even the floor felt loud, with everyone's shoes on the tile. I did what I came to do and then stood by a pillar with a coffee, just watching. It was strangely impressive, like weather. After twenty minutes my ears were ringing. I'm not sensitive to noise normally, but I was relieved to get outside. It's a place that makes you appreciate silence for a week.","words":173,"p3":[{"q":"How can people consider others' feelings when chatting in public?","a":"By keeping their voice at a level where only the person next to them can hear. And by not using a speakerphone, which is the single most annoying habit on public transport."},{"q":"How would people usually respond to noises in your country?","a":"Mostly by ignoring it, which is a kind of tolerance. Occasionally someone will say something, but confrontation is rare. People complain online instead."},{"q":"Which area is exposed to noise more, the city or the countryside?","a":"The city, obviously, traffic, construction and crowds. But the countryside has its own noises, animals, machinery, and at night it can be surprisingly loud."},{"q":"What kinds of noises are there in our life?","a":"Traffic, construction, neighbours, phones, and the general hum of a city. And then natural sounds, which we tend to notice only when they're gone."},{"q":"Should children not be allowed to make noise under any circumstances?","a":"No, that's unreasonable. Children are loud because they're learning to be in the world. There are places where quiet matters, like a library, and they can learn that."},{"q":"Do you think it is good for children to make noise?","a":"Yes, within reason. Noise usually means play, and play is how children learn. Silence in a room full of children is more worrying than shouting."}]},"喜欢的城市自然之地":{"mat":"M6 滨江公园","text":"The natural place I keep going back to is a stretch of riverside park about twenty minutes from my flat, on the west bank of the river. It's nothing dramatic, a long paved path, some grass, a lot of plane trees and a few benches that have seen better days. But there's a section where the path runs right along the water, and if you look one way you see the towers and if you look the other way it's just river and sky. That's rare here. I go two or three times a week, usually early in the morning before work. Sometimes I run, sometimes I just walk with a coffee. At weekends I'll go with a friend, but on weekdays it's usually just me. What I like is that it's the one place in the city where nothing is expected of you. Nobody's selling you anything, there's no queue, no screen. You can sit on a bench for half an hour and it counts as doing something. In a city this dense, that's worth a lot.","words":178,"p3":[{"q":"Which is more important, protecting the environment or developing the economy?","a":"In the short term they conflict, in the long term you can't have one without the other. A damaged environment eventually stops the economy, so it isn't really a choice."},{"q":"Do you think staying in a natural place would help change people’s mood?","a":"Yes, and most people can feel it within an hour. Your breathing slows down and you stop checking your phone. There's research on this, but you don't need it."},{"q":"Do you think there should be many natural places in cities?","a":"As many as possible. They're not a luxury, they're infrastructure. A city without green space is cheaper to build and much worse to live in."},{"q":"What can governments do to encourage people to spend more time in nature?","a":"Make it easy, which means free parks within walking distance and safe paths. Telling people to go outside doesn't work if the nearest park is a bus ride away."},{"q":"Do you think people should spend more time in nature?","a":"Yes, particularly children. Most of the benefits are boring and well known, better sleep, less anxiety, and yet almost nobody does it."},{"q":"What kind of people do you think enjoy being in nature?","a":"People who grew up near it, mostly, and older people. Younger people often say they enjoy it but don't actually go."}]},"攒钱买特别物品":{"mat":"M7 相机","text":"I saved up for about eight months to buy a second-hand camera, and it's still the most deliberate purchase I've ever made. It was a small mirrorless one, nothing professional, but for me it was a lot of money. I wanted it because I'd got into taking photos on my phone and hit the limits of what a phone could do, since everything looked flat, especially in the evening. I wanted to actually learn how exposure worked. The saving wasn't dramatic. I just moved a fixed amount into a separate account every month and stopped buying coffee on the way to work, which sounds trivial but added up. I also sold an old games console I never used. The day I bought it I felt slightly sick, to be honest, because it was a lot of money for a hobby. That passed within a week. What I remember most is the first evening I took it out and everything I pointed it at looked the way I'd imagined. I still use it. The saving was part of the pleasure, actually.","words":181,"p3":[{"q":"Should people spend time on charity, or just money?","a":"Both, but time is usually more useful and much rarer. Money can be replaced, an afternoon of somebody's attention can't."},{"q":"What kind of things do people usually save money for?","a":"A house, mostly, and then big things like a car, a wedding or a trip. Younger people also save for a deposit on a flat, which in this city takes years."},{"q":"Is it easier to spend money or save money?","a":"Spending is easier, and it's designed to be. Every app is built to remove the moment where you might change your mind."},{"q":"Do you think it is easier to teach teenagers how to manage money when they are around 16 or 17 years old?","a":"Easier than at any other age, I'd say, because they want things and they can earn a little. It's the age when money stops being abstract."},{"q":"Should parents teach their children to spend money freely or to save money?","a":"Both, and in that order. A child who's never allowed to spend will grow up either fearful or reckless. The useful lesson is choosing between two things you want."},{"q":"Should schools teach children how to manage money from a young age?","a":"Yes, because most parents don't. Schools teach trigonometry and nothing about interest, and interest affects everyone's life."}]},"小时候学到的新技能":{"mat":"M5 爷爷教象棋","text":"The skill I learned as a child that I still use is chess. I must have been about seven. My grandfather taught me, on a battered wooden board with a couple of pieces missing. He wasn't a good teacher in the formal sense. He never explained anything, he just played and let me lose. I lost for about two years, which sounds cruel but taught me to look at the whole board instead of just my own pieces. I learned it the slow way, playing him every Sunday afternoon after lunch. He'd pause before moving and tap the piece twice, and after a while I understood that the tapping meant he was about to do something I'd regret. How I felt about it at the time was mostly frustration. I remember being genuinely angry at losing. But somewhere in there I started to enjoy the thinking, and by the time I was ten I could beat him occasionally, which felt enormous. I still play now, online, and I still hear the tapping.","words":172,"p3":[{"q":"Is it important for everyone to have a specialized skill?","a":"It's useful but not essential. What's more important is being good at learning, because the skill you have at twenty may not exist at forty."},{"q":"Can a child learn skills from another child?","a":"Constantly, and often faster than from an adult. Children explain things in the language of someone who's just learned it themselves."},{"q":"Do you think it is easier for children to learn new skills than for adults?","a":"For physical things, yes, coordination and language especially. For anything requiring patience or self-discipline, adults have the advantage."},{"q":"What are the differences between the way adults learn and the way children learn?","a":"Adults need reasons, children just copy. Adults are also embarrassed to be bad at something, which slows them down considerably."},{"q":"What are the differences between learning in a group and learning by yourself?","a":"A group gives you feedback and competition, alone you can go at your own pace. Most people need a bit of both, and the balance changes with the skill."},{"q":"What skills should children learn before they go to school?","a":"How to ask for help, how to wait, and how to be in a group without hitting anyone. Reading and counting can come a little later."}]},"喜欢拍照的人":{"mat":"M9 胶卷小林","text":"The person I know who's genuinely into photography is a friend called Lin, who I met through work about five years ago. She shoots film, which is unusual now. She carries a small nineteen-seventies camera in her bag at all times and has a fridge shelf full of film. She mostly takes photos of ordinary things, doorways, people waiting for buses, her own kitchen, rather than landmarks or scenery. She says she isn't interested in places that are already beautiful. I've been out shooting with her a couple of times and it's a slow process. She'll stand in front of a wall for ten minutes waiting for the light to change, and I get bored within three. How I feel about her is a mixture of admiration and slight envy. She sees things I walk straight past. And she isn't doing it for an audience, which I find rare, because she rarely posts anything. Her photos make me notice my own neighbourhood. That's a real gift.","words":167,"p3":[{"q":"Some people don't mind others walking into their shot when they're taking photos.","a":"I'm the opposite, I'd wait for the frame to clear. But it depends what you're shooting. For a landscape, people in it are noise, whereas for a street scene they're the point."},{"q":"Why do tourists like taking photos while they are travelling?","a":"Partly to remember, partly to prove they were there. And increasingly to show other people, which is a different motivation."},{"q":"Do you think it’s ok to take photos everywhere?","a":"Not everywhere. Not in changing rooms, obviously, not in some religious places, and not of strangers' children. Beyond that, most places are fair game."},{"q":"What kind of photos do people often take?","a":"Food, selfies, and whatever's in front of them, plus a great many photos of the same landmark from slightly different angles."},{"q":"Why are some people keen on taking photos?","a":"Because looking through a camera is a way of paying attention. It gives you a reason to look at things properly."},{"q":"When do people like taking photos?","a":"On special occasions, obviously, but also whenever something feels like it's about to end. Half the photos on my phone are of ordinary afternoons I didn't want to forget."}]},"尊敬的比你年长的人":{"mat":"M5 爷爷","text":"The older person I admire most is my grandfather, who's in his eighties. I grew up in the same building as him, so he's always been around. He was a carpenter, and after he retired he kept working, not for money, just because he couldn't sit still. People in the neighbourhood would bring him broken chairs and he'd fix them for nothing. Things we do together are simple. We play chess, which he always wins, and we watch football, which he takes far too seriously. He also likes me to read out the news to him, because his eyes aren't good, and then he tells me what's wrong with the world. What I admire is that he's never once complained about his circumstances. He had a hard life by any standard and he talks about it lightly, as if it happened to somebody else. I'd like to be that calm at his age. I'm not close.","words":156,"p3":[{"q":"What kinds of things do the elderly remember for a long time?","a":"Things from their twenties, mostly, and things that were difficult. My grandfather can't remember last week but can describe a meal from nineteen sixty-five."},{"q":"What sports are popular among older people?","a":"Walking, tai chi, swimming and table tennis. Anything low-impact and social, and in this country square dancing counts as well."},{"q":"What do old people usually do in their daily life?","a":"Exercise early, shop for food, cook, look after grandchildren, and watch a great deal of television. Many also look after their own parents, which people forget."},{"q":"Why is there a generation gap between the young and the old?","a":"Because they grew up in completely different conditions and the technology changed under them. It isn't stubbornness, it's different information."},{"q":"Has old people's life quality improved when compared to the past?","a":"Materially, enormously, better housing, healthcare and pensions. Whether they're less lonely is a different question, and I'm not sure the answer is yes."},{"q":"What can young and old people learn from each other?","a":"Older people can teach patience and how to do things with your hands. Younger people can teach them how to use a phone, and sometimes how to be less afraid of change."}]},"好的购物服务":{"mat":"独立素材 · 修表店","text":"A while ago I had a really good experience in a small watch repair shop, of all places. It's in an old shopping arcade near my flat, a tiny place with glass counters and a man in his sixties behind them. I'd taken in a watch I'd bought years before that had stopped, expecting to be told it wasn't worth repairing. He opened the back, looked at it for a while and told me exactly what was wrong, then explained that the repair would cost about a third of what the watch was worth. He also said, unprompted, that the strap would need replacing within a year, but that I could wait. What impressed me was that he didn't try to sell me anything. He could easily have told me to buy a new one. He fixed it in three days and charged exactly what he'd quoted. I felt oddly loyal afterwards. I've been back twice since, once just to buy a battery. That's all service really is, I think, being told the truth.","words":174,"p3":[{"q":"Can consumption drive economic growth?","a":"It can, and in most developed economies it does most of the work. The risk is that it's driven by debt, which works until it doesn't."},{"q":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of shopping online?","a":"The advantages are price and choice, you can compare everything in ten minutes. The disadvantages are that you can't touch anything, and returning things is a hassle."},{"q":"What are the differences between online shopping and in-store shopping?","a":"Online is a search, in-store is an experience. You go online knowing what you want, and you go to a shop to find out what you want."},{"q":"Why do some people not like shopping in small shops?","a":"Because you feel obliged to buy, and because the prices aren't visible. Some people find being served uncomfortable."},{"q":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of shopping in small shops?","a":"You get real advice and something nobody else has. You also pay more, have less choice, and the shop may not exist next year."},{"q":"Why are shopping malls so popular in China?","a":"Because they're not just shops, they're places to be. Air conditioning, food, cinemas, and somewhere to take children on a hot day."}]},"擅长做手工的人":{"mat":"独立素材 · 邻居木匠","text":"The person who's good with their hands is a neighbour of mine, a retired carpenter called Uncle Chen. He makes furniture, mostly small things now, stools, shelves, a shoe rack for his daughter. He works in the yard behind the building, and in summer you can hear the plane going back and forth all afternoon. He uses ordinary hand tools, a plane, chisels and a saw, and hardly any machines. He's good because he's done it for fifty years and because he's patient. I watched him make a small stool once and he must have checked the legs twenty times, holding it up to the light and running his thumb along the joints. He said a stool that wobbles is worse than no stool. He gave me a small bookshelf for my flat, made from wood he'd kept for years. The joints are so tight you can't see them. I think people like him are becoming rare, and it's a real loss. Everything I own is machine-made, and none of it feels like that shelf.","words":176,"p3":[{"q":"Does art, like music or theater, have an impact on society?","a":"Yes, but slowly and indirectly. It changes what people find normal to talk about, which is more influential than any single work."},{"q":"Is it good to have creative people in a team?","a":"Usually, as long as someone else is managing the deadline. Creative people are good at the beginning and unreliable at the end."},{"q":"What impact can artistic buildings have on society?","a":"They give a place an identity, and they make people look up. Whether that's worth the cost is a fair question, but the effect is real."},{"q":"Do children these days still like doing arts and crafts?","a":"Some do, but far fewer than before, because screens are easier. The ones who do tend to be obsessive about it."},{"q":"Why do some children enjoy making things by hand?","a":"Because they end up with something that exists. Most of a child's day is instructions and worksheets, and making something is the opposite."},{"q":"Do you think art classes are important for children?","a":"Yes, though not mainly for artistic reasons. They teach you to keep going at something with no right answer, which is a rare skill."}]},"和朋友度过的愉快夜晚":{"mat":"M9 火锅夜","text":"One evening that stands out is a dinner at a friend's flat last winter, which turned into the kind of night you talk about for months. There were five of us, all teachers or ex-teachers, and it was supposed to be a quick hotpot on a Friday. It started at seven and I got home at two in the morning. What we did was mostly cook and talk. Someone brought far too much food, three kinds of mushrooms, hand-cut lamb, a bag of dumplings we never got to. We sat around the table for hours with the pot going, and the conversation moved from work to our parents to a very long argument about whether it's better to rent or buy. It was enjoyable because nothing was planned. No restaurant, no timetable, nobody checking their phone. And because everyone there had known each other long enough to be honest. I remember walking home in the cold feeling unusually content. Those evenings are getting rarer as we all get busier.","words":171,"p3":[{"q":"Why do people need friends?","a":"Because you need someone who isn't obliged to like you. Family is given, friends are chosen, and that makes their opinion worth more."},{"q":"Do older people prefer spending time with family or with friends?","a":"Family, in this country, but I think that's partly expectation rather than preference. The happiest older people I know have friends they see without their family."},{"q":"Do young people prefer spending time with family or with friends?","a":"Friends, almost universally, between about fourteen and thirty. Then family starts to matter again, usually when children appear."},{"q":"What are the differences between young people’s leisure time activities and those of old people?","a":"Young people go out and spend money, older people stay in and spend time. Also, young people do things in groups and older people in pairs."},{"q":"What can we do to bring family members closer together?","a":"Eat together regularly, and do something that isn't watching a screen. Most families only meet at meals, and half of those are in restaurants."},{"q":"How do people get along in a big family?","a":"By having routines and by not discussing certain things. In a big family you learn which topics are safe, and peace depends on respecting that."}]},"浪费时间的活动":{"mat":"独立素材 · 刷短视频","text":"The activity I do regularly that I'd honestly call a waste of time is scrolling short videos on my phone. I do it mostly at night, in bed, which is the worst possible time. It starts with ten minutes while I'm waiting for something, and then it's an hour and I couldn't tell you a single thing I watched. The reason I do it is boredom, mostly, and tiredness. It's the easiest thing in the world, with no decision to make and nothing to prepare. After a long day of teaching, choosing a book feels like effort and scrolling doesn't. Why I think it's a waste of time is that it gives nothing back. I don't remember it, I don't learn anything, and I don't even enjoy it much while it's happening. It just removes an hour. What's annoying is that I know all this and still do it. I've tried deleting the app, and I lasted four days. The honest answer is that the problem isn't the videos, it's me being too tired to choose anything better.","words":178,"p3":[{"q":"Why do some people refuse to abide by rules?","a":"Sometimes because the rule is stupid, sometimes because they've never seen it enforced. Most rule-breaking isn't rebellion, it's convenience."},{"q":"What kinds of things make people feel pressured?","a":"Deadlines, money, and other people's expectations. The worst is all three at once, which is basically being thirty."},{"q":"Will you continue doing something when you are aware that it’s a waste of time?","a":"Sometimes, yes, and I know it's irrational. Scrolling is the obvious example. Knowing it's a waste doesn't stop it, because at that moment I'm too tired to choose better."},{"q":"How do you balance life and work?","a":"Badly, on the whole, but I've got better by making work end at a fixed time. The trick isn't balancing each day, it's protecting a few evenings a week absolutely."},{"q":"Is it good to have a daily routine?","a":"For most people yes, because it removes decisions. The danger is that a routine can become a way of not noticing anything."},{"q":"Do you think stress is important in people's lives?","a":"A certain amount, yes, it's what makes you prepare. The problem is the kind that doesn't switch off, which isn't stress any more, it's just damage."}]},"拥挤地方":{"mat":"独立素材 · 外滩跨年","text":"A really crowded place I've been to is the Bund on New Year's Eve, which I did once and will never do again. I went with two friends, about three years ago. The plan was to watch the light show on the buildings across the river and then get something to eat. What actually happened was that we stood in a crowd so dense we couldn't move for about forty minutes. You weren't walking, you were being carried. I remember a woman next to us holding a child above her head so he could breathe, and a man who'd climbed onto a barrier just to see. We did see the show, briefly. But the whole thing was so uncomfortable that none of us enjoyed it. What struck me was how quickly a crowd stops being a group of people and becomes a kind of force. It isn't aggressive, just completely indifferent to individuals. We left before midnight and ate noodles in a nearly empty shop. That was the best part of the night.","words":173,"p3":[{"q":"Do you think it is a good trend to have more big cities?","a":"Up to a point. Big cities are efficient and generate opportunity, but beyond a certain size the costs, housing, transport, pollution, grow faster than the benefits."},{"q":"Do you think there are some people who enjoy crowded places?","a":"Definitely, and I'm not one of them. Some people find crowds energising, and for them a quiet street feels dead."},{"q":"Why do people go to crowded places?","a":"Because that's where the thing they want is, a concert, a market, a famous view. Or because everybody else is going, which is a real reason."},{"q":"Will people use bikes more or less in cities in the future?","a":"More, I think, as electric bikes get cheaper and cities get more congested. Weather and the safety of the roads are the only real limits."},{"q":"Will there be more or less green space in cities in the future?","a":"More in theory, because everyone wants it, and less in practice, because land is worth too much. The compromise is small parks and green roofs."},{"q":"Which cities are overcrowded in your country?","a":"The first-tier ones, Beijing, Shanghai, Shenzhen, Guangzhou. They're not overcrowded in terms of space exactly, they're overcrowded in terms of housing and transport."}]},"学习并喜欢历史的人":{"mat":"M5 读史的爷爷","text":"The person I know who really loves history is my grandfather, though he'd never call himself a historian. He reads constantly, mostly Chinese history, and he reads the same books more than once. His flat is full of them, with notes in the margins in very small handwriting. He also watches documentary series and argues with them out loud, which is quite funny to listen to. He learned it mostly by himself. He left school early, so everything he knows comes from reading, and he's got an astonishing memory for dates and names. He'll tell you which year a railway was built and then explain why it mattered. Why he loves it, I think, is that it makes ordinary life feel connected to something bigger. He grew up in a village and lived through enormous changes, and history is how he makes sense of them. I find it slightly humbling. I studied history at school and remember almost none of it, while he can reconstruct a whole century from memory. He's the reason I read history now.","words":177,"p3":[{"q":"Besides big historical events, what else is important when learning history?","a":"How ordinary people lived, what they ate, how they earned money, what they were afraid of. That's usually more revealing than the battles."},{"q":"Do you think people should learn ancient history before learning modern history?","a":"Not necessarily, but it helps to go in order, because each period explains the next. Starting in the middle is why so many people find history confusing."},{"q":"Is it important to learn how people in the past lived their lives?","a":"Yes, because it makes them real. Otherwise history is just names and dates, and nobody remembers names and dates."},{"q":"How do children learn history before they attend school?","a":"From stories, films and grandparents, mostly. It's unreliable, and it's how everyone starts."},{"q":"At what age do you think people should begin to learn history?","a":"Around seven or eight, through stories rather than dates. The dates can come later, and usually do."},{"q":"Do you think children should begin learning history from an early age?","a":"Yes, but as narrative, not as a subject. If it starts as a list of years, they'll never come back to it."}]},"教你新技能的人":{"mat":"M4 跑友老魏","text":"The person who taught me a genuinely new skill as an adult was a colleague of mine, who got me into running properly. I'd been jogging badly for years, too fast, too far, injured every spring. He'd run several marathons and offered to come out with me once. What he taught wasn't really running, it was slowing down. He made me run at a pace where I could still hold a conversation, which felt ridiculous at first, and he taught me to build distance gradually instead of going all out. He also made me buy proper shoes, which I'd been resisting for years. We ran together for about three months, twice a week, mostly at six in the morning. He talked the whole way, which was the point, because if I could answer him I wasn't going too fast. Within a year I ran a half marathon. I'd never have done that alone. What I feel for him is straightforward gratitude. He gave me something I use two or three times a week, and it's changed how I feel physically.","words":180,"p3":[{"q":"Why is self-discipline important when learning a new skill?","a":"Because progress is boring and uneven. You improve for two weeks and then plateau for two months, and only discipline gets you through the plateau."},{"q":"What are the differences between learning from a teacher and learning by oneself?","a":"A teacher corrects you before the mistake becomes a habit. Alone, you can go faster and learn the wrong thing very thoroughly."},{"q":"How do adults learn a new skill?","a":"Slowly, in short sessions, and with a lot of self-consciousness. Adults also need to understand why something works, whereas children just repeat it."},{"q":"How can parents and teachers teach children new skills?","a":"By doing it in front of them and letting them join in, rather than instructing. Children copy far more than they obey."},{"q":"Where can children learn new skills?","a":"At home, at school, in clubs, and increasingly online. The best place is usually wherever an adult is doing something and doesn't mind being interrupted."},{"q":"Why should children learn new skills?","a":"Because learning itself is the skill. A child who's learned three unrelated things knows how to start the fourth."}]},"不喜欢的电影":{"mat":"独立素材 · 超级英雄片","text":"A film I really didn't enjoy was one of those big superhero films, which I watched about a year ago because a friend insisted. I saw it at the cinema, on a Saturday, with a group of four. I'd been told it was the best one in the series, which raised my expectations. It was about the usual thing, a threat to the world, a team that has to assemble, a great deal of destruction. It was two and a half hours long and I think about an hour of it was fighting. What I didn't like was that nothing felt at stake. Buildings fall over and nobody's really hurt, and you know how it'll end from the first ten minutes. The jokes were also the same jokes. I felt a bit cheated, honestly, and slightly out of step, because everyone else came out saying how good it was. I said it was fine. I don't mind spectacle, but I need characters I care about. Give me a small film about two people in a kitchen any day.","words":178,"p3":[{"q":"Do people’s movie preferences change as they get older?","a":"Yes, broadly from spectacle to character. At fifteen you want things to explode, at thirty-five you want to watch two people talk in a kitchen."},{"q":"Should documentary films be completely truthful?","a":"They should be honest about their argument, which isn't the same thing. Total objectivity doesn't exist in film, but misleading editing is a different matter."},{"q":"Can movies help people better understand the cultural background of a country?","a":"Yes, better than almost anything else, because they show how people behave rather than what they believe. You learn what a family dinner looks like."},{"q":"Are movies more likely to help people become more creative than books?","a":"Books, I'd say, because you have to build the images yourself. Film gives you everything, which is its strength and its limit."},{"q":"What are the differences between watching movies at home and in a cinema?","a":"At home you can pause, which means you never concentrate fully. In a cinema you can't leave, and that forced attention is most of the experience."},{"q":"What kinds of movies are the most popular in China?","a":"Comedies, action films and anything with a big national story. Also family films at holiday time, which is when cinemas make their money."}]},"不喜欢的短途旅行":{"mat":"M2 郊区教学点","text":"The short trip I take regularly and don't enjoy is my weekly journey out to a teaching centre on the edge of the city. I go every Saturday morning, and it's about an hour and a half each way. I go because I teach a class there, it's a decent chunk of my income and the students are good. What I don't like is everything around it. I have to leave at half seven, the metro is packed with people going to work on a Saturday, and the last part is a bus that comes every twenty minutes. The centre itself is in a shopping mall with no windows, so I spend four hours in artificial light. By the time I get home it's five o'clock and the day's gone. I've tried to make it pleasant, I listen to podcasts and bring a book, but it's fundamentally dead time. The honest reason I keep doing it is money. I've thought about dropping it and I probably will next year, once I've replaced those students with ones closer to home. For now it's a trade I've decided to make.","words":188,"p3":[{"q":"W hat problems does it cause for transportation when everyone commutes at the same time?","a":"The system has to be built for the peak, which means it sits half empty the rest of the day. And everyone arrives at work already tired."},{"q":"What are the benefits of travelling abroad?","a":"You find out that your way of doing things isn't the only way. That's uncomfortable and useful, and you can't get it from a book."},{"q":"Which is better for knowing more about a country? Travelling or reading books about it?","a":"Books give you the background, travel gives you the texture. Alone, travel gives you a lot of impressions and no understanding."},{"q":"How much time do you think people should spend on a trip abroad?","a":"At least two weeks, otherwise the travelling takes up the whole trip. A month is better, but almost nobody can do that."},{"q":"Who prefers travelling abroad? The young or the old?","a":"The young, by a wide margin, because they have fewer obligations and more appetite for discomfort. Older people prefer comfort, and often prefer their own country."},{"q":"Do Chinese people like travelling abroad?","a":"Increasingly yes, and in enormous numbers. Short-haul destinations in Southeast Asia are the most popular, because they're cheap and close."}]},"受欢迎的人":{"mat":"M2 同事黄老师","text":"The popular person I want to describe isn't a celebrity, it's a colleague of mine, a teacher called Ms Huang. She's in her fifties and has been teaching for nearly thirty years. She's the kind of person who knows everybody, the cleaners, the security guards, the parents, the newest teachers. When you walk down the corridor with her it takes twice as long because she stops to talk to people. As a person she's warm but not soft. She'll tell you exactly what she thinks, and somehow people don't mind, because she's obviously on their side. She's also extremely funny in a dry way. I see her mostly at work, in the staff room, where there's always a small group around her desk at lunch. Why she's popular, I think, is that she remembers things about people. She'll ask how your mother's operation went, three months later. Nobody does that. It isn't a technique, either. She just pays attention, and people can feel it. That's why everyone trusts her.","words":169,"p3":[{"q":"Why do people want to be popular?","a":"Because it's a form of security. If people like you, you get more chances and fewer obstacles, and that's hard to ignore."},{"q":"Is it easier to become popular nowadays?","a":"Easier to be widely known, harder to be genuinely liked. Attention is cheap now, and everyone can see how much of it is manufactured."},{"q":"What are the qualities of being a good teacher?","a":"Knowing the subject, obviously, but mostly being able to explain the same thing five different ways without losing patience."},{"q":"Do you think good teachers are always popular among students?","a":"No, and the best ones often aren't. A teacher who makes you work hard is unpopular in October and remembered for twenty years."},{"q":"Is it important for a teacher to be popular?","a":"It helps, because students work for people they like. But it's a means, not an aim, and teachers who chase it tend to go soft."},{"q":"Why are some students popular in school?","a":"Usually because they're good at something visible, sport, jokes, or simply being confident. Being kind helps, but it's slower."}]},"钦佩的运动员":{"mat":"独立素材 · 苏炳添","text":"The sportsperson I admire most is the Chinese sprinter Su Bingtian. What I know about him is fairly simple. He's from Guangdong, he isn't tall for a sprinter, and in twenty twenty-one he ran a hundred metres in nine point eight three seconds, which made him the first Chinese athlete to reach an Olympic hundred-metre final. What he's like in real life, from interviews, is unusually thoughtful for an athlete. He talks about technique and about the science of running rather than just about effort. He also studied at university while competing, and he's now teaching. His achievement isn't only the time. It's that he did it at twenty-nine, which for a sprinter is ancient, and that he did it after everyone had decided Asian athletes couldn't compete at that level. I admire him because he solved a problem nobody thought could be solved, and he did it with patience rather than talent. He changed his starting technique completely at an age when most people are retiring. I'm not a big sports fan, but I find that genuinely inspiring.","words":182,"p3":[{"q":"Why are there so few top athletes?","a":"Because it needs talent, years of training and the right body, and all three at once is rare. Most people have one of the three."},{"q":"What is the most popular sport in your country?","a":"Football in terms of watching, and badminton or running in terms of doing. Basketball is enormous among younger people."},{"q":"Is it easy to identify children’s talents?","a":"No, and people get it wrong constantly, usually by mistaking early physical development for talent. The late developers are the ones who get missed."},{"q":"Is talent important in sports?","a":"It's the entry ticket, but it doesn't decide the outcome. At the top everyone is talented, and what separates them is work and nerve."},{"q":"What qualities should an athlete have?","a":"Discipline, obviously, and the ability to lose without falling apart. Also a certain stupidity about pain, which I mean as a compliment."},{"q":"Should students have physical education and do sports at school?","a":"Yes, and it should be taken as seriously as maths. It's the only subject that teaches you how to deal with your own body for the next sixty years."}]},"喜欢的公共建筑":{"mat":"独立素材 · 市图书馆","text":"A public building I really enjoy visiting is the main city library, which is about half an hour from me. I found out about it years ago, when I was a student and needed somewhere quiet to work. It's a big modern building, concrete and glass, with a huge reading room on the top floor and windows that run the whole length of the wall. The tables are long wooden ones with proper lamps, and there's a courtyard in the middle with trees. What I like about it is the silence. Not total silence, you can hear chairs and pages and someone coughing, but a particular kind of quiet that you don't get anywhere else. Nobody talks on the phone, nobody's trying to sell you anything. Everyone there has agreed to behave in the same way. I go maybe once a month now, usually to work through something I've been putting off. I get more done in three hours there than in a whole day at home. It's also free, which in this city is close to a miracle.","words":179,"p3":[{"q":"Why do people visit historic buildings?","a":"To feel the scale of the past. A photograph of a cathedral doesn't tell you what it's like to stand in one."},{"q":"Should modern buildings replace traditional buildings?","a":"Sometimes, when the old building is unsafe or useless. But mostly no, because once they're gone you can't get them back, and the new thing could be built anywhere."},{"q":"Is it better to live in an apartment than in a house?","a":"In a dense city, yes, because a house wastes land and you end up driving everywhere. If you have children and space, a house is better."},{"q":"Is it important to protect historic buildings? Why?","a":"Yes, because they're the only physical evidence of how people used to live. And because a city with no old buildings feels like it has no memory."},{"q":"Why is it hard to protect historic buildings?","a":"Because the land underneath is worth a fortune, and maintenance costs money nobody wants to spend. Preservation is always cheaper in theory than in practice."},{"q":"Why do some people prefer to live in the countryside?","a":"Space, quiet and cheaper housing. The cost is a car, a long commute and much less to do in the evening."}]},"印象深刻的聚会":{"mat":"独立素材 · 表哥婚礼","text":"The party that impressed me most was my cousin's wedding, about two years ago, in a hotel on the edge of the city. It was held for the wedding itself. The formal part was in the afternoon and the party was in the evening. There must have been two hundred and fifty people, most of whom were relatives I hadn't seen since I was a child. What impressed me wasn't the scale, it was how the family organised it. My aunt had made a spreadsheet of the seating, and there was a whole group of cousins whose only job was to look after the older guests. There was a slideshow of photos going back forty years, and when my grandmother's picture came up, the whole room went quiet. I'm not usually sentimental about these things. But there was something about seeing three generations in one room, all arguing about the same things they'd argued about for decades. I ended up sitting with an uncle I barely knew, talking about fishing until midnight. I left thinking that families are mostly held together by occasions like that, and that we don't have enough of them.","words":193,"p3":[{"q":"When do people like to hold a party?","a":"For anything with a date attached, birthdays, weddings, festivals, and increasingly for no reason at all, which I think is healthier."},{"q":"Do you think people nowadays prefer attending big parties or small gatherings with close friends? Why?","a":"Small gatherings, I'd say, and I think that's partly a reaction to how much of our social life is now public. People want a room where nothing is being recorded."},{"q":"Are there any differences between how young people and older people celebrate events with parties?","a":"Young people care about the atmosphere and the photos, older people care about who's there. The food matters more to the older group."},{"q":"What special events are celebrated in your country?","a":"The Spring Festival, weddings, the first month of a baby's life, and graduation. Also the mid-autumn festival, which is mainly about food."},{"q":"Do you think it’s a waste of money for governments to celebrate national events?","a":"Some of it, yes. A parade that costs more than a hospital is hard to defend. But there's a case for shared occasions that aren't commercial."},{"q":"What festivals are the most important for people in your country?","a":"The Spring Festival, without question, because it's the one that makes everyone travel home. Then mid-autumn, which is about family."}]},"有健康习惯和作息的人":{"mat":"M4 跑友老魏","text":"The person with genuinely healthy habits that I know is a friend of mine, Wei, who I met through running. We met about four years ago at a park where a group of people run on Sunday mornings. He was the one who talked to the new person, and we've been running together since. What he does is not extreme, which is why I trust him. He goes to bed at eleven and gets up at six, he cooks most of his own food, and he runs four times a week but never more. He doesn't drink much and he doesn't own a car, he cycles everywhere, including in the rain. He also takes one full day off exercise every week, deliberately. What I feel about him is a kind of respect mixed with mild guilt. He isn't preachy at all, he never tells anyone what to do. But after a weekend with him I always sleep better. He's in his forties and looks ten years younger, which he'd say is luck. I think it's just consistency.","words":177,"p3":[{"q":"Does the government play an important role in promoting healthy lifestyles in a community?","a":"A big one, and more effective than telling people to try harder. Parks, bike lanes, food labelling and school meals do more than any campaign."},{"q":"Do you think it is important to live in a community where people have healthy lifestyles?","a":"It helps enormously, because behaviour is contagious. If everyone around you walks after dinner, you walk after dinner."},{"q":"How can people relieve stress in daily life?","a":"Exercise, sleep, and having one person you can complain to honestly. Alcohol is popular and makes it worse."},{"q":"What effect does stress have on health?","a":"It affects almost everything, sleep, digestion, blood pressure, and how often you get ill. Long-term stress is one of the most damaging things there is."},{"q":"Which group pays more attention to health, young people or old people?","a":"Old people, because they have to. Young people pay attention to how they look, which isn't the same thing."}]},"读起来开心的书":{"mat":"独立素材 ·《人类简史》","text":"The book that made me genuinely happy while reading it was Sapiens, by Yuval Noah Harari. It's popular science, really, a history of our species from the Stone Age to now. It isn't a novel, but it reads like one. The chapters are short and each one makes a big argument, about money, about religion, about why we ended up farming when it made our lives harder. I read it about three years ago, mostly on the metro, and I remember looking up at the other passengers and thinking about the book's claim that everything we organise our lives around is a shared story. Money, countries, companies, none of it physically exists. Why it made me happy is that it rearranged my head. I'd finish a chapter and want to argue with someone about it. Very few books do that to me. I don't agree with all of it, and some of it is probably too neat. But it made the world feel more interesting than it had the week before, and that's worth a lot.","words":176,"p3":[{"q":"What kinds of books are popular in China?","a":"Practical ones, self-improvement, business, and history presented as storytelling. Fiction sells well, but non-fiction dominates the bestseller lists."},{"q":"Do you think people should read the original book before watching the movie?","a":"Before is better, because afterwards you can't stop comparing. But reading it at all matters more than the order."},{"q":"Are success books useful to everyone?","a":"No, and most of them are one idea stretched to two hundred pages. They're useful to people who are already inclined to act."},{"q":"Do you think people today read less than before? Why?","a":"Less long-form reading, certainly, though probably more words overall. Phones didn't remove reading, they broke it into pieces."},{"q":"Why do some people prefer watching movies to reading books?","a":"Because it takes two hours instead of ten, and it requires nothing from you. Also, films are social in a way that books aren't."},{"q":"How can parents help children develop a love of reading?","a":"By reading in front of them, and by not making it a punishment. A house with books and no pressure works better than any scheme."}]},"帮助他人":{"mat":"独立素材 · 地铁站","text":"A time I helped someone was at a metro station about a year ago, and it was such a small thing that I still think about why it stayed with me. There was an older man by the ticket machines, clearly stuck. He was holding a phone in one hand and a banknote in the other, and people were walking around him. He'd been there long enough that he'd stopped trying. I asked if he needed help. He wanted to buy a ticket, but the machines only took phone payment and he didn't have the app. The ticket office was closed for renovation. So I bought him a ticket on my phone, and then, and this was the part that took time, I walked him to the platform and explained which line to take and where to change. He was going to a hospital to see his wife. He tried to give me the banknote. I said no, and he insisted, so I took it and put it back in his bag while he wasn't looking. I felt useful in a very ordinary way. And slightly ashamed that I'd walked past two other people before I stopped.","words":197,"p3":[{"q":"Should children be taught to be kind to others?","a":"Yes, but by practice rather than instruction. Being told to be kind is meaningless, being asked to help carries weight."},{"q":"Should people be very kind when they help others?","a":"Not necessarily warm, just useful. Some of the most helpful people I know are quite blunt."},{"q":"Can kids provide any help to parents?","a":"Plenty, small chores, looking after siblings, noticing when someone's upset. Children are more capable than adults usually admit."},{"q":"Should parents give children advice? Why?","a":"Yes, but sparingly, or it becomes noise. Advice works best when the child has already half-decided something."},{"q":"What kind of advice should parents give to their children?","a":"Practical things they can't learn alone, like how to handle money or how to say no. Not opinions about their friends."},{"q":"Should parents help their kids with their homework?","a":"Help with the conditions, the time and the quiet, rather than the content. Doing the homework together teaches dependence."}]},"听不感兴趣的话":{"mat":"M2 同事讲车","text":"The time I remember most clearly is a lunch with a colleague who spent about forty minutes telling me about his new car. It was at work, in the staff room, and I'd made the mistake of asking how his weekend was. He's a genuinely nice man and he'd just bought a car he was proud of, so he started at the beginning, the research, the test drives, the dealer, the discount he negotiated. I'm not interested in cars at all. I don't own one and I find the details meaningless. But he was so pleased that I couldn't bring myself to change the subject. Why I kept listening is partly politeness, and partly that he doesn't have many people to tell. His family lives in another city, and work is his whole social life. What I felt was a bit of both, bored, and then guilty about being bored. Somewhere in the middle I realised he wasn't really telling me about the car, he was telling me he'd done something difficult and it had worked out. We're better colleagues now. I still don't care about cars.","words":187,"p3":[{"q":"What kind of topics do young people like to talk about today?","a":"Work, money, relationships and whatever they've just watched. Much of it happens in group chats rather than in person."},{"q":"What would happen if the listener didn’t listen carefully?","a":"The conversation collapses into two monologues. People notice, even if they don't say so, and they stop telling you things."},{"q":"How can we tell if a listener is listening carefully?","a":"By whether they ask a question about what you said. Nodding means nothing, a follow-up question means everything."},{"q":"How can a teacher tell if students are listening in class?","a":"By asking them to explain it back. Faces are unreliable, and some of the best listeners look completely blank."},{"q":"What do you think about talking with people who have different opinions?","a":"It's necessary and it's exhausting. The useful version is when both people are genuinely curious, the useless version is when both are performing."},{"q":"Do most young people in your country like to talk with elderly people?","a":"Not really, mostly because of the questions about marriage and salary. Where the gap is smaller, they get on fine."}]},"不会用的科技产品":{"mat":"M7 相机","text":"The piece of technology I couldn't get to grips with was a mirrorless camera I bought a few years ago, which had far more settings than I expected. I'd assumed it would be like a phone with a bigger lens, and it absolutely wasn't. There were dials for aperture and shutter speed, a menu system with about eight levels, and a manual that was a hundred pages long and seemed to be written for people who already knew. What made it hard was that the automatic mode produced worse photos than my phone, so I couldn't just ignore the settings. If the pictures were going to be better, I had to understand what I was doing. I did learn, eventually, mostly from video tutorials rather than the manual. It took a couple of months before I could change a setting without taking my eye off the screen. How I felt about it was frustrated at first, then slightly addicted. I still don't use half the features. What I learned is that easy to use mostly means fewer choices, and cameras deliberately give you more.","words":184,"p3":[{"q":"Are there any disadvantages of using technology?","a":"Several. Attention is the main one, then privacy, then the fact that skills disappear when you stop using them. I couldn't navigate without a phone now."},{"q":"Do you think it is good to use technology in education?","a":"Yes, if it's doing something a teacher can't, like showing a three-dimensional shape or giving instant practice. If it's just a screen replacing a book, it adds nothing."}]},"有创造力的朋友":{"mat":"M9 设计师小林","text":"The creative friend I want to describe is Lin, who I've known for about five years. We met through work. She designed some materials for a course I was teaching, and we got on immediately because she argued with me about the layout and turned out to be right. She's a graphic designer by trade, but the creative part is what she does outside work. She makes things constantly, prints, small books, badges, strange objects out of cardboard. She uses whatever's around, paper, old packaging, fabric scraps from a tailor near her flat, and occasionally a proper printing press at a studio she borrows. Why I think she's creative is less about talent and more about how she sees things. She'll look at a discarded box and see a lamp. She also finishes things, which most creative people I know don't, because she'll make something rough in an afternoon and give it away. I feel slightly envious, honestly, and grateful. She's the person who reminds me that making something is more satisfying than consuming something.","words":175,"p3":[{"q":"Do you think art classes are important for children?","a":"Yes, for the same reason as before, they teach you to work without a right answer. That's the opposite of most of school."},{"q":"Do you think creativity is important for everyone?","a":"Yes, though not only in the artistic sense. Most jobs involve solving a problem nobody's written down, and that is creativity."},{"q":"Does creativity come from nature or nurture?","a":"Both, but nurture has more room than people think. Almost every creative adult had an adult who let them make a mess."},{"q":"Do you think there are too many advertisements on TV in your country?","a":"Yes, and they're longer than the programmes. Most people under forty have stopped watching broadcast television entirely because of it."},{"q":"Do you have a favourite advertisement?","a":"One for a milk brand from my childhood, mostly for the jingle. I couldn't tell you what the ad looked like, only the tune."},{"q":"Have you ever bought something because of an advertisement?","a":"Yes, a pair of running shoes after a video ad. They were fine, but I bought the feeling, not the shoes."}]},"喜欢或不喜欢的高建筑":{"mat":"独立素材 · 上海中心","text":"The tall building I want to talk about is the Shanghai Tower, which I like, though with some reservations. It's mostly offices, with a hotel near the top and an observation deck. It's in the financial district, in a cluster with two other very tall towers, which makes the area look like a graph. What it looks like is the interesting part. It's a twisted tower, so the whole shape spirals as it goes up, and the surface is glass with a double skin that's supposed to save energy. From a distance it looks soft, almost like fabric, which is odd for something that height. Why I like it is that it doesn't look like a machine for making money. Most towers that size are just boxes, and this one has an idea behind it. The observation deck is genuinely worth it too, because you can see the river bend. Why I have reservations is that it's a symbol of the kind of building I'm not sure cities actually need. But I'd rather have a beautiful tower than an ugly one.","words":181,"p3":[{"q":"Are there many tall buildings in your country?","a":"Yes, in the big cities they're everywhere, and they're getting taller. Outside the city centres it's a different picture, low-rise housing for miles. So it really depends on where you are."},{"q":"What are the differences between those tall buildings in your country?","a":"The old ones are concrete, identical, built fast and cheap, and they look their age. The new ones are glass and steel, mixed use, offices on the bottom and flats above. Almost a different species."},{"q":"Why are different places laid out and designed differently?","a":"Mostly climate and history. A city built before cars grew up dense and walkable; one built after cars spread out. Money matters too, since towers are expensive."},{"q":"What are the advantages of living in tall buildings?","a":"Space, mainly, because you use a small footprint for a lot of people. And you get light, views, and often shops or a gym in the same building."},{"q":"Why do some people like to remodel and decorate their homes themselves?","a":"Because it's cheaper than moving, and it's the only way to get exactly what you want. There's also real pleasure in making a place yours."},{"q":"What kind of interior design style do most people like?","a":"Simple and light, I'd say. White walls, wood, not much clutter. The heavy ornate style is popular with a certain age group, but most younger people want calm."}]},"有趣视频":{"mat":"M3 成都面馆","text":"The video I want to talk about is a short documentary I watched on my phone a couple of weeks ago, while I was having lunch at home. It was about a noodle stall in Chengdu that's been run by the same family for over thirty years. The camera just follows the owner through a normal morning, kneading dough, setting up, arguing with his regulars. Every so often he says something quietly profound without seeming to notice. I watched it because it appeared in my feed and I'd meant to scroll past. What stopped me was that there was no music and no commentary, just the sound of the street. What I felt afterwards was a bit envious, honestly. He's done one thing for three decades and seems completely at ease with it. At one point he says the street outside has changed beyond recognition but his job hasn't, because people still want the same bowl of noodles. I watched it twice, which I never do. It made me think about how much time I spend chasing things that don't really matter.","words":182,"p3":[{"q":"What kind of videos do people in your country like to watch?","a":"Short comedy, food, sports clips, and a lot of news explainers. Cooking and repair videos are huge too, because people watch them with a purpose rather than just to pass time."},{"q":"Which is more helpful, watching videos or reading books?","a":"Videos are better for anything you have to see done, like a technique. Books are better for anything you have to think about slowly. They're tools for different jobs."},{"q":"What skills can people learn from watching videos?","a":"Cooking, basic repairs, software, and languages. Some of it is genuinely useful. A fair amount is just the feeling of having learned something."},{"q":"Are there any differences between the videos that young people and old people like to watch?","a":"Yes, quite big. Younger people go for short and fast, older people for longer news and documentaries. Though that gap is closing as everyone gets used to short form."},{"q":"Are there any differences between the videos that young men and young women like to watch?","a":"Less than you'd think. Gaming and sport draw more men, beauty and lifestyle more women, but the overlap is large. The algorithm pushes everyone the same thing eventually."},{"q":"What makes a video go viral online?","a":"Something that lands in the first three seconds, makes you feel one strong emotion, and is easy to copy. Novelty plus a simple format beats high production every time."}]},"去过的无聊地方":{"mat":"M2 郊区商场","text":"A boring place I've been to, and unfortunately go to quite often, is a big shopping mall out near where I teach at weekends. It's in a newly built district on the edge of the city, surrounded by identical apartment blocks and empty roads. I go there because the teaching centre I work at is on the fourth floor, so I'm in the building for hours every Saturday. What I do there is teach, eat something from the food court, and walk around between classes. That's the whole experience. Why I think it's boring is that nothing about it is real. Every shop is a chain you've seen a hundred times, the air conditioning is the same, there are no windows on the teaching floor, and you could be in any city in the country. There's no street life, no market, nobody doing anything unexpected. What makes it worse is that it's designed to be inoffensive, so there's nothing to dislike and nothing to remember. I usually leave feeling I've lost a day. That's a strange thing for a building to do to you.","words":184,"p3":[{"q":"Why do most children think education is boring?","a":"Because they're made to sit still and listen, and children aren't built for that. Also because they can't see why any of it matters yet."},{"q":"Why aren’t young people willing to listen to the experiences of older people?","a":"Partly because the world they grew up in doesn't exist any more. Advice about jobs and marriage from forty years ago can sound like a different country."},{"q":"What can people do when they feel bored?","a":"Reach for a phone, mostly. Which is fine for ten minutes and bad for a whole afternoon. Walking somewhere helps more than people expect."},{"q":"Why are some teachers’ classes boring? Are there any solutions?","a":"Because the teacher does all the talking and the students do all the listening. The fix is simple but tiring, give them a task and let them get it wrong."},{"q":"Why do some young people feel bored when talking with old people?","a":"Because the questions repeat, and the references don't overlap. Young people have nothing to say about people who died before they were born."},{"q":"Do most people think news about celebrities is boring?","a":"For many, yes, because it's the same three stories about the same five people. But it isn't boring to the people who follow it, obviously."}]},"早起经历":{"mat":"M4 晨跑","text":"A time I got up really early was last autumn, when I decided to run before work for a week to see if it was possible. I was up at five, which for me is the middle of the night. I'd laid everything out the night before, shoes, clothes, a banana, because I knew any decision at that hour would end with me going back to bed. What I did was run along the riverside path for about forty minutes while it was still dark, then come home, shower and start teaching. The first two days were horrible. By Thursday something clicked. Why I got up early was practical. My evenings are full of classes and by nine at night I'm finished, so morning was the only slot left. And the path is empty at that hour, which is the only way I can run without dodging people. How I felt about it was surprised, mostly. I'd always believed I wasn't a morning person. It turns out I just hadn't tried properly. I still do it twice a week. Not five times. Twice.","words":183,"p3":[{"q":"Do you know anyone who likes to get up early?","a":"My father, yes, he's up at half five without an alarm. He's done it for thirty years, so I'm not sure it's a choice any more."},{"q":"Why do people get up early?","a":"Work, mostly, and commuting. Then there's the group who get up early on purpose, for the quiet hour before anyone needs them."},{"q":"What kinds of occasions need people to arrive early?","a":"Flights, exams, interviews, hospital appointments. Anything where being late costs you money or the whole opportunity."},{"q":"Why do some people like to stay up late?","a":"Because it's the only time nobody can ask them for anything. There's a real pleasure in being awake while the house is silent."},{"q":"Is it good to arrive early in any situation?","a":"Usually, yes, but not absurdly early. Arriving forty minutes before a meeting just makes everyone awkward."},{"q":"What kind of people like getting up early?","a":"Parents, older people, runners, and anyone with a long commute. Also quite anxious people, in my experience."}]},"喜欢在家 /花园种菜的人":{"mat":"独立素材 · 母亲阳台","text":"The person I know who loves growing things is my mother, who has turned a small balcony into something between a garden and a farm. She grows tomatoes in pots, spring onions in old plastic boxes, chillies, mint, and a lemon tree that has never produced a lemon but is treated with enormous respect. She also grows beans up a frame she built herself out of bamboo. How she does it is mostly improvisation. She saves seeds from vegetables she's eaten, uses rice water as fertiliser, and moves the pots around through the day to chase the sun. She talks to them, which she denies. Why she loves it, I think, is that it gives her results she can see. Her job is administrative and nothing ever feels finished, whereas a tomato either ripens or it doesn't. I benefit from it, obviously. In summer I go over with a bag and come back with more than I can eat. What I find interesting is that she's far more relaxed doing that than anything else. Half an hour with the pots and she's a different person.","words":185,"p3":[{"q":"What are the advantages of growing vegetables or flowers at home?","a":"It's cheap, the food is fresher, and you know what's been put on it. There's also the calming side, which is probably why retired people take it up."},{"q":"Do many people grow vegetables or flowers at home in your country?","a":"Yes, more than I expected. Balconies are full of pots, and there are small allotments on the edge of the city. Usually it's the older generation who start it."},{"q":"Is it easy to grow plants at home?","a":"Herbs are easy, tomatoes are manageable. Anything that needs a lot of sun or space is not, and pests will find it before you do."},{"q":"Why do some people like to grow plants?","a":"Because growth is slow and visible, which is oddly satisfying. You can see you've done something, and it doesn't argue with you."},{"q":"Why do some people prefer to grow their own fruits and vegetables instead of buying them from the market?","a":"Taste is the main one, a tomato from a garden really is different. Then safety, because people worry about what's sprayed on shop vegetables."},{"q":"Do you think students should learn to grow plant?","a":"Yes, but as one small project rather than a subject. If a child grows one bean and eats it, they understand food differently for life."}]},"想颁布的新法律":{"mat":"独立素材 · 限制短视频","text":"The new law I'd like to see is a strict limit on how short-video apps can target children, along the lines of what some countries have started doing. The law would ban personalised recommendation feeds for under-eighteens, cap daily use at something like forty minutes, and require the platforms to default to chronological order rather than an algorithm. It would also make the companies publish what their algorithms actually optimise for. What changes it would bring are fairly obvious. Children would be bored more often, which I think is healthy. Parents would stop fighting about screen time every evening. And the platforms would lose a chunk of their most valuable users, which is precisely why the law is needed. Whether it will be popular is doubtful. Parents would support it, teenagers would hate it, and the companies would fight it for years. So it would be controversial and then people would adjust, the way they did with seatbelts. How I came up with it was through teaching. I see students who can't concentrate for twenty minutes, and it isn't their fault. I feel strongly about it. I also know it's the kind of law that's hard to enforce.","words":200,"p3":[{"q":"What rules should students follow at school?","a":"Punctuality, respect for other people's things, and handing work in on time. Beyond that, most rules are about safety rather than learning."},{"q":"Do people in your country usually obey the law?","a":"Mostly, yes, in daily life. Traffic is where it breaks down, because enforcement is patchy and everyone copies everyone else."},{"q":"What kinds of behavior are considered as good behavior?","a":"Honesty, courtesy, queuing properly, and helping someone who's clearly struggling. Small things, but they're what makes a place livable."},{"q":"Do you think children can learn about the law outside of school?","a":"They can, but it's scattered, from family, films, the news. School is where it gets structured, which is why the basics belong there."},{"q":"What are the benefits for people to obey rules?","a":"Predictability, mostly. If everyone follows the same rules, you don't have to negotiate every interaction. That saves an enormous amount of energy."},{"q":"How can parents teach children to obey rules?","a":"By doing it themselves, and by being consistent. A rule that's enforced on Monday and ignored on Wednesday teaches children that rules are negotiable."}]},"发小":{"mat":"独立素材 · 邻居陶","text":"The friend from my childhood I still know is a boy called Tao, who lived in the flat below mine. We met because our mothers knew each other, which is how friendships started in that building. We were about five, and for the next ten years we were basically inseparable. What we often did together was be outdoors. We played football in the car park until someone shouted at us, collected bottle caps, built things out of cardboard, and later spent entire summers playing video games in his room with the curtains closed. What made me like him was that he was completely unimpressed by everything. He never tried to be cool, and he laughed at himself before anyone else could. That's rare at fifteen. We're still in touch, though we live in different cities now. We talk maybe twice a year and it's exactly the same conversation as always. I think childhood friends are the only people who knew you before you decided who to be.","words":167,"p3":[{"q":"Do you still keep in touch with your friends from childhood? Why or why not?","a":"Two or three, and not regularly. It's the kind of friendship where you can go a year without talking and pick up in five minutes."},{"q":"How important is childhood friendship to children?","a":"Very, I'd say. It's the first relationship where they choose each other, so it teaches them something family can't."},{"q":"What do you think of communicating via social media?","a":"It's convenient and it's low effort. What it loses is tone, so jokes land wrong and small things turn into arguments."},{"q":"Do you think online communication through social media will replace face-to-face communication?","a":"No, I don't think so. People still want to sit down with someone. What's changed is that we now do the boring parts online and save meeting for the good parts."},{"q":"What's the difference between having younger friends and older friends?","a":"Older friends give advice and perspective. Younger ones give energy and keep you from getting stuck in your own habits."},{"q":"Has technology changed people's friendships? How?","a":"Yes, mostly by widening the circle and thinning it. I know hundreds of people online and speak properly to maybe six."}]},"想从事医疗行业的人":{"mat":"M2 学生一涵","text":"The person I know who wants to go into medicine is a former student of mine, a girl called Yihan, who's now at university. I taught her maths for about two years, from when she was fifteen. She told me in our second month that she wanted to be a doctor, and unlike most fifteen-year-olds she never changed her mind. She started thinking about it, she said, when her grandmother was ill and the family spent weeks in and out of hospital. What struck her wasn't the doctors' knowledge, it was how one of them explained things to her mother. She decided she wanted to be the person who explains. What she'd like to do is paediatrics, though she's keeping an open mind. She's currently studying medicine, which is a six-year course here, and she says the first year is mostly chemistry and very little medicine. Why she'd be good at it is that she's patient and quite unshakeable. She once sat with a problem for an hour without getting frustrated. I admire that kind of certainty. I didn't know what I wanted at eighteen.","words":188,"p3":[{"q":"Do you think being a doctor is easy or difficult?","a":"Difficult. Long hours, high stakes, and you carry other people's bad days home with you. The training alone takes a decade."},{"q":"Do you think learning biology is interesting for children?","a":"Yes, if it's taught with real bodies and experiments. Taught as vocabulary from a textbook, it's one of the dullest subjects there is."},{"q":"Why do some children want to become doctors?","a":"Respect, mostly, and the wish to help. Quite often there's a doctor in the family, so it feels like a known path rather than a guess."},{"q":"Do you think governments should put a large amount of money into medical research?","a":"Yes, but not only that. Research is expensive and slow, and hospitals are short of beds and nurses. The money has to be split."},{"q":"Why is some doctors’ pay high and others’ low?","a":"Speciality, mostly. Surgeons and specialists earn more than general practitioners, and private practice pays better than public."},{"q":"Do you think doctors should be paid more?","a":"Junior doctors in public hospitals, yes. The pay is poor for the hours, and that's why so many leave."}]},"拥有成功商业的人":{"mat":"独立素材 · 面包店","text":"The person I know with a successful business is a friend of a friend, a woman called Zhang who runs a small bakery. I got to know her about three years ago, through a friend, at the bakery itself. It's a tiny place with four tables and a window onto the street, and it always smells incredible. Why she started it is the interesting part. She was an accountant for ten years, hated it, and started baking at weekends as a way of staying sane. Then people began ordering cakes, then a small shop came up for rent, and she took the risk at thirty-five. What she does is proper bread, the slow kind that takes two days, plus a few cakes. Nothing else. She refuses to add sandwiches or coffee, which everyone told her to do. Why I think it's successful is that she's refused to grow. It's profitable enough for her and two staff, she knows most customers by name, and she closes on Mondays. The queue on Saturday mornings is out of the door. She says that isn't the point, but I think she likes it.","words":190,"p3":[{"q":"Why do some people start their own business?","a":"Control, mostly, and the feeling that the effort goes to themselves. Some have a specific idea they can't let go of, and a few just couldn't work for anyone else."},{"q":"Should governments provide financial support to start-ups?","a":"Yes, but as loans rather than grants. Cheap credit helps good businesses grow. Grants tend to fund whichever founder writes the best application."},{"q":"Do most people prefer shopping at big stores or small stores?","a":"Big stores for groceries and anything boring, small ones for service and things you want advice on. Small shops are coming back for exactly that reason."},{"q":"What makes a business successful?","a":"Repeat customers and cash flow. A business can survive bad luck but not running out of money, and it dies without people coming back."},{"q":"What makes a business fail?","a":"No cash, a market that isn't really there, and growing faster than the systems can handle. Most failures are boring, not dramatic."},{"q":"Is it easy to set up a new business in your country?","a":"Registering one is easy, it takes a day. Keeping it alive past the second year is the hard part, and most don't."}]},"近期改变的计划":{"mat":"M3 旅行改期","text":"A plan I had to change recently was a trip to Japan that I'd been organising for about six months. The original plan was to go in April, during the cherry blossom, for ten days with two friends. We'd booked flights and the first two nights, and I'd made a spreadsheet of the rest, because I'm that kind of person. What made me change it was work. Two months before, the centre I teach at asked me to take on a group course that ran through exactly those two weeks, and it was the kind of offer I couldn't really refuse. The new plan was much smaller, a five-day trip in autumn instead, with only one friend, because the other couldn't move his dates. How I felt about the change was annoyed at first, and then fine. The autumn trip turned out to be better, honestly. Fewer tourists, better food, and none of the pressure to see the famous trees. What I learned is that the itinerary was doing more harm than good. The best day was the one with nothing planned.","words":183,"p3":[{"q":"Do people often change their plans?","a":"Constantly, and usually for small reasons. Someone gets ill, the weather turns, a meeting overruns. The bigger the plan, the less often it changes."},{"q":"Would you tell others if you change your plan?","a":"Yes, if someone else is affected. If it's only my own afternoon, I don't bother telling anyone. Cancelling on people is where the etiquette lives."},{"q":"Why do you think parents still make plans for their children nowadays?","a":"Because the stakes feel enormous now, and because they can. University choice, tutoring, even which subjects to take, none of it is left to chance."},{"q":"How does technology help people make plans?","a":"Mostly by removing the guesswork. Maps tell you how long a trip takes, calendars warn you about clashes, and everything is searchable. Whether that makes plans better is another question."},{"q":"What kind of plans do people often make?","a":"Trips, weddings, moves, and study. Then a huge number of small ones, like what to cook or when to shop. Most planning is housekeeping."},{"q":"Do you think people like the process of making plans more, or the moment of carrying them out?","a":"Making them, I suspect. Anticipation is cheap and unbroken. The moment of carrying them out involves other people, weather, and things going wrong."}]},"在团队中工作":{"mat":"M2 重编课程","text":"A time I worked in a group that I remember clearly was redesigning the maths course for a small school I was working at. There were four of us, three teachers and the head of department, and we had about six weeks before the new term. What we did was split the syllabus, write new materials and then review each other's work every Friday. My job was the algebra section and the practice questions. The problems we faced were mostly about standards. One colleague wanted everything to look identical and polished, and I thought that was a waste of the six weeks. There was a fairly tense meeting where I said so. Why we worked in a group is that no single person could write a year's course in six weeks, and the school wanted consistency between classes. We solved it by agreeing on a template and then letting everyone work inside it, which is the obvious answer that took us two weeks to find. What I took from it is that most group conflict is about unstated assumptions rather than real disagreement.","words":183,"p3":[{"q":"Why do some people prefer to work by themselves?","a":"Because they can hold the whole thing in their head and move fast. Some people also find the negotiation exhausting, and would rather just do the work."},{"q":"What should a leader do to make team members want to follow him or her?","a":"Two things. Be clear about what success looks like, and then get out of the way. People follow someone who protects them from chaos."},{"q":"Should students learn to do group work?","a":"Yes, definitely. Nobody's job is solo any more, and group work is a skill you can only learn by doing it badly first."},{"q":"What group tasks are there in schools?","a":"Presentations, science projects, sports, and group essays. In my experience the group essay teaches the least, because one person usually writes it all."},{"q":"What advantages are there for students experiencing teamwork at school?","a":"They learn to explain themselves, to compromise, and to notice when someone's drifting. All three matter more at work than most exam content."},{"q":"How can you tell if a person is a good leader?","a":"By watching what happens when things go wrong. Anyone looks calm when it's going well. A good leader takes responsibility and doesn't look for someone to blame."}]},"重要决定":{"mat":"M2 入行选择","text":"An important decision I made was choosing to teach rather than to continue studying maths. I was in my final year at university, and the obvious path was a master's degree, then probably research or finance. Everyone expected it, including me. How I made my decision was slow and not very rational. I took a part-time job teaching at a small centre to pay rent, and found that I looked forward to it more than my own coursework. I gave myself a term to see whether that lasted, and it did. The results were that I turned down a place on a master's programme, which my supervisor thought was a mistake, and started teaching full time. Why it was important is that it decided the shape of the next ten years. It's also the only decision I've made where I ignored good advice and turned out to be right. I don't recommend ignoring advice as a strategy. But I do think you learn something from noticing what you actually enjoy, rather than what you're supposed to.","words":177,"p3":[{"q":"Do you think children sometimes have to make important decisions?","a":"Occasionally, but not many. Which school to go to, whether to move. Most of the time adults decide and the child is informed."},{"q":"What important decisions do teenagers need to make after graduation?","a":"What to study, and whether to study at all. Then where to live and who to spend time with. The first one sets the direction of everything after it."},{"q":"Who can children turn to for help when making a decision?","a":"Parents first, then teachers, then friends. The useful thing is having one adult who won't panic, because teenagers often just need someone to hear the whole mess out loud."},{"q":"Do you think advertisements can influence our decisions when shopping?","a":"Yes, heavily, and more than people admit. Half the time you walk in wanting a specific brand and you'd struggle to say why."},{"q":"Do you think the influence of advertising is good?","a":"Partly. It tells you what exists, which is genuinely useful. The problem is it also decides what you want, and that's not the same thing."},{"q":"How do people usually make important decisions?","a":"Badly, usually. People decide with a feeling and then build the reasons afterwards. The ones who do it well tend to write things down."}]},"喜欢的现场体育赛事":{"mat":"独立素材 · 足球赛","text":"A live sports event I really enjoyed was a football match I went to about two years ago, in the city stadium. It was a league game, nothing glamorous, on a cold Saturday evening. I went with my cousin, who's a proper fan and knows every player. What I liked wasn't really the football. It was the crowd. Forty thousand people singing the same song badly, and the way the noise rose before a corner. When the home team scored, the whole stand moved, which is slightly frightening and completely exhilarating. The game itself finished one all, which I'm told was a poor result. Why I liked it is that I'd only ever watched football on television, where you hear the commentary and see the replays and it's basically a different thing. In the stadium you have no idea what's happening half the time, but you feel it. I've been back once since. I wouldn't go every week, because it's expensive and cold, but I understand now why people do.","words":170,"p3":[{"q":"Why do some people like to watch sports events?","a":"The atmosphere, mostly. A goal feels different when thirty thousand people react at once. On television you see more, but you feel less."},{"q":"Where do people normally watch sports events?","a":"At home, for most games. Then a minority go to the stadium regularly, and a lot watch in bars or with friends."},{"q":"What are the advantages of watching sports events online?","a":"You can watch anything, from anywhere, and pause it. The cost is that you already know the result if you waited, and the crowd noise is fake."},{"q":"What sports matches are suitable for children to attend?","a":"Swimming, athletics, table tennis, anything without contact and without a lot of shouting. Football is fantastic but the language in the stands isn't for children."},{"q":"Why do some people spend a lot going to other countries to watch sports events?","a":"Because it's a reason to travel with people you like. The match is the excuse, and often it's the least memorable part of the trip."},{"q":"What sports games are popular in your country?","a":"Football, basketball, badminton and table tennis. Football is the one that produces actual arguments, which tells you how much people care."}]},"特别场合的食物":{"mat":"独立素材 · 饺子","text":"The food I want to talk about is dumplings, which in the north of China are eaten at almost every important occasion. The special event is the Spring Festival, though they also turn up at weddings and on the first day of winter. The idea is that the shape resembles an old gold ingot, so eating them is a wish for money in the coming year. My family also puts a coin in one, and whoever gets it is supposed to have good luck. How they're made is a family operation. Someone makes the dough, someone rolls the wrappers, and everyone sits around filling and folding. The filling is usually pork and cabbage, though my grandmother did a version with prawns that nobody has managed to copy. Why people eat them on that occasion is partly tradition and partly practical. It's a food that takes hours and several pairs of hands, so it only makes sense when everyone's together and there's nothing else to do. That's what I like about it. The eating is almost secondary to sitting around the table arguing about how thick the wrappers should be.","words":189,"p3":[{"q":"Why are there special foods on special occasions or events?","a":"Because food is the easiest way to mark a day as different. It's cheap, it's shared, and everybody has to sit down for it."},{"q":"What are the differences between everyday food and festival food?","a":"Festival food takes time and isn't everyday food. Everyday food is quick and forgettable, festival food is slow and has a story attached."},{"q":"Are there any differences between the food people eat today and the food people ate in the past?","a":"Yes, a lot. People eat far more meat and sugar now, and far more imported things. But the dishes themselves have barely changed."},{"q":"Do people today prefer eating at home or in a restaurant?","a":"Home, I think, for ordinary meals. Restaurants are for occasions, and people cook at home more than they did ten years ago, partly for money and partly for health."}]},"擅长学习和说语言的人":{"mat":"独立素材 · 朋友 Anna","text":"The person I know who's genuinely good at languages is a friend called Anna, who I met at university. She's from here originally but she's lived in three countries, and she speaks Mandarin, English, Spanish and reasonably good Japanese. What's annoying is that she doesn't think she's good at it. How she learns is not through classes. She watches television in the language with subtitles, and she talks to people almost immediately, badly, which most of us can't bear to do. She also keeps a notebook of phrases she's heard real people use, not textbook sentences. What languages she can speak, as I said, four, and she's started on Portuguese, which she claims is cheating because it's close to Spanish. How I feel about her is a mixture of admiration and frustration, because she makes it look like a personality trait rather than work. In reality she probably spends two hours a day on it. What I've learned from her is that the barrier isn't memory, it's being willing to sound stupid for a few months. I'm still working on that.","words":181,"p3":[{"q":"Are there many people who can speak foreign languages in your country?","a":"Quite a lot, actually, mostly younger people. English is standard, and a few take Japanese or Korean because of films and music."},{"q":"Does speaking other languages help at work?","a":"Yes, in most international companies it's the difference between being in the room and hearing about the meeting afterwards."},{"q":"Do people learn any languages other than English?","a":"Some do, Japanese and Korean especially, and French or German for people who want to study in Europe."},{"q":"Why is it easier for children to learn new things than for adults?","a":"Because children aren't embarrassed. They'll say a word wrong fifty times and not care. Adults stop at the first mistake."},{"q":"How do people learn new things?","a":"By copying first, then by using it badly in public. Instruction helps, but the people who get good are the ones who tolerate being ridiculous."},{"q":"What is the most important thing for learning a language well?","a":"Using it, I'd say. You can study grammar for years and still freeze. Ten minutes of real conversation teaches more."}]},"遇到的科技问题":{"mat":"M7 硬盘损坏","text":"The biggest technological problem I've faced was a hard drive failing, which took about three years of teaching materials with it. It happened about two years ago, on a Sunday evening, while I was preparing for the week. The laptop started making a clicking noise and then wouldn't start at all. Everything was on that machine, lesson plans, worksheets, exam papers I'd written, and about four hundred photos. How challenging it was, was mostly emotional. The drive was replaceable in a day. The files weren't. I sat there for an hour doing nothing, which is not a useful response. I solved it in two ways. A colleague recommended a data recovery shop, and they got back maybe seventy per cent of it, mostly the documents but not the photos. And then I bought an external drive and set up automatic backups, which I should have done years earlier. What I learned is that the disaster isn't the technology failing, it's discovering you had no plan for it. I now keep three copies of anything that matters, and I check the backup every few months. The photos are the only thing I really regret.","words":193,"p3":[{"q":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of AI?","a":"It's fast and endlessly patient, which is genuinely useful. The problems are that it makes things up convincingly, and nobody knows where the answers came from."},{"q":"Do you think people today should learn about AI technology?","a":"Yes, at least enough to know what it can't do. Using it without that is like driving without knowing what the brakes are for."},{"q":"Should children learn to use AI?","a":"The tool, yes, but with supervision. Children pick it up instantly, which is exactly why they need someone to point out when it's wrong."},{"q":"How can AI help in our lives?","a":"It drafts, translates, summarises and searches. The unglamorous uses save the most time, like turning a long document into five bullet points."},{"q":"Do you think students are overly reliant on AI?","a":"Some are. It's much easier to ask than to think, and the answer arrives finished, which removes the struggle that learning depends on."},{"q":"What can teachers do to stop students from relying too much on AI?","a":"Set tasks that have to be done in the room, and ask about the process rather than the product. If they can't explain their own answer, it isn't theirs."}]},"名人出演的广告":{"mat":"独立素材 · 运动品牌","text":"An advertisement with a famous person that I actually remember is one for a sports brand, featuring a well-known basketball player. I see it online mostly, on video platforms, and occasionally on screens at metro stations. It's been running for a while in various versions. What the advertisement is about is not really the shoes. It shows him training alone at five in the morning, missing shots, and then the actual game where he scores. There's very little dialogue, just a lot of breathing and the sound of a ball. How I feel about it is slightly manipulated, and I know it. The logic is that if I buy the shoes I'm buying some of that discipline, which is obviously nonsense. But the ad is well made enough that I don't mind being manipulated. What I find interesting is that these ads almost never show the product clearly. They sell a mood. I didn't buy the shoes. I did, however, go for a run the next morning, so it worked on some level.","words":174,"p3":[{"q":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of advertisements?","a":"They tell you things exist, which is useful, and they create a lot of noise. The bigger problem is that they sell a feeling rather than a product."},{"q":"Why are many advertisements endorsed by celebrities? How useful are they?","a":"Because a familiar face borrows trust. They work well for things you can't judge yourself, like insurance or skincare, and badly for anything you can check."},{"q":"What is the most important factor in an advertisement?","a":"Clarity. If I can't tell what's being sold in three seconds, the rest doesn't matter. Cleverness is usually a way of hiding a weak product."},{"q":"Why are some advertisements boring?","a":"Because they're made by committee. Every good idea gets sanded down until nobody can object, and the result is something nobody remembers."},{"q":"Is advertising important for a company? Why?","a":"Yes, for anything new. Without advertising a new product is invisible, no matter how good. For a product people already want, it matters much less."},{"q":"Which is more effective, online advertising or offline advertising?","a":"Online, because you can measure it and target it. Offline still works for things people need to see physically, like cars or property."}]},"推荐旅行过的地方":{"mat":"M3 成都","text":"The place I'd recommend to anyone is Chengdu, in the southwest, which I visited about two years ago. It's a big city, but it doesn't feel like one. The pace is slower, there are tea houses everywhere, and people genuinely seem to sit around for hours doing nothing, which is unusual in this country. What I saw and did was mostly eating and walking. I went to a teahouse in a park where people were having their ears cleaned and playing cards, and I sat there for three hours. I ate hotpot twice, badly the first time. I also went to see the pandas, early in the morning, which is the only sensible time. Why I'd recommend it is that it's the one Chinese city I've been to where I stopped checking the time. It's also cheap compared with Shanghai, and the food is better. One warning, don't go in summer. The humidity is genuinely unpleasant, and the city sits in a basin so there's no breeze at all. Go in autumn. Everything's better in autumn.","words":176,"p3":[{"q":"Where do people in your country often go for holidays?","a":"Domestic trips mostly, to the coast or to the mountains. Younger people go abroad when they can, usually to Japan, Thailand or somewhere in Europe."},{"q":"What is the ideal length for a holiday?","a":"A week, I'd say. Long enough to stop rushing, short enough that you still like the people you're with."},{"q":"How do people usually plan holidays?","a":"Loosely, these days. Book the flights and the first two nights, then decide as you go. Over-planning a holiday is a common mistake."},{"q":"Is it important to plan a holiday ahead?","a":"Some planning, yes, because the good places fill up. But leave gaps. The best parts of most trips are the bits nobody scheduled."},{"q":"Why do many countries try to attract people to visit?","a":"Because it brings money in and creates jobs, particularly in places with nothing else. It's one of the few exports a small town can sell."},{"q":"How do people decide when to travel?","a":"By price, mostly. Flights are cheapest outside school holidays, so families with children are locked into the worst two weeks of the year."}]},"喜欢拜访但不想住的家":{"mat":"独立素材 · 乡下老屋","text":"The home I like visiting but wouldn't want to live in is my aunt's place in a village about two hours outside the city. It's an old house with a courtyard, a well, and a kitchen that's basically outdoors. The walls are thick and it's cool in summer without any air conditioning. There's a persimmon tree in the yard and chickens that wander in and out. Why I like visiting is that it's the opposite of my flat in every way. It's quiet, there's nothing to buy, and time behaves differently. My aunt cooks enormous meals and refuses to let anyone help. I sleep better there than anywhere. Why I wouldn't like to live there is mostly practical. The nearest hospital is half an hour away, there's no proper internet, and in winter it's genuinely cold because the house was built before insulation existed. And I'd have no work. What I realised is that I like it as a visitor precisely because I'm a visitor. Two days is perfect. Two weeks would turn it into a different place. It's a good reminder that the things that make a place charming are often the same things that make it inconvenient.","words":199,"p3":[{"q":"Do Chinese people like to visit others’ homes?","a":"Yes, but it's become less common in the cities. People are busy and flats are small, so meeting in a restaurant is easier. In smaller places it's still completely normal."},{"q":"What do Chinese people do when they visit others?","a":"Sit down, drink tea, eat far too much fruit, and talk. Guests are not expected to help, which can be awkward if you're the kind of person who wants to."},{"q":"What kind of place do people in your country like to live in?","a":"Somewhere with light and space, close to transport. In the cities that means a flat, and the priority is usually the location rather than the size."},{"q":"What's the difference between homes in cities and those in the countryside?","a":"Country houses are bigger and have gardens, but they're further from everything. City homes are smaller and noisier, and worth far more per square metre."},{"q":"What kind of gifts do people usually bring when they visit others?","a":"Fruit, tea, milk, or something for the children. Expensive gifts would embarrass people, so practical things are safer."},{"q":"How often do you visit your relatives or friends?","a":"Less than I should. Relatives maybe once a month, friends more often but usually outside the home."}]},"包含动物的故事或书":{"mat":"独立素材 ·《夏洛的网》","text":"The book with animals in it that I'd talk about is Charlotte's Web, which I read as a child and then again, out loud, to a student. It's about a pig called Wilbur who's due to be killed, and a spider called Charlotte who decides to save him by writing words in her web. There's also a rat, a goose, and a great many farmyard animals. What the book is about, underneath, is friendship and mortality. Charlotte saves Wilbur's life and then dies herself, which as a child I found shocking and as an adult I find quite beautiful. She doesn't ask for anything. Why I read it the second time is that a student of mine was learning English and needed something not too difficult, and I remembered it. Reading it aloud is a different experience. The sentences are simple but they aren't childish. What I think of it is that it's one of the few children's books that doesn't lie. It doesn't pretend death doesn't happen. It just says that being useful to someone is worth something. I cried at the end, both times.","words":186,"p3":[{"q":"Should schools teach children about animals?","a":"Yes, at least the basics. Children are naturally interested, and understanding how other species live is part of understanding the world."},{"q":"Some people think pets should not be kept in cities. What do you think?","a":"I don't agree. Cities are where most pets live happily. The problem isn't the city, it's owners who leave dogs alone all day in a small flat."},{"q":"Many people regard pets as members of their family. What do you think?","a":"I think it's fine as long as it isn't used as an excuse. Treating a pet as family is good for the animal. Treating it as a substitute for human relationships is where it gets strange."},{"q":"Do many people keep pets in your country?","a":"Very many, and the number went up sharply over the last ten years. Cats especially, because they suit small flats and busy schedules."},{"q":"What are the advantages of keeping a pet?","a":"They give you a reason to get up and something to touch. Walking a dog is also a social life, whether you wanted one or not."},{"q":"Why do people always tell children stories with animals?","a":"Because it's easier for a child to accept a lesson from a talking fox than from an adult. The animal carries the moral so the story doesn't have to lecture."}]},"保护环境的法律":{"mat":"独立素材 · 垃圾分类","text":"The environmental law I know best is the household waste sorting regulation, which came into force in Shanghai a few years ago. What it requires is that households separate rubbish into four categories, dry, wet, recyclable and hazardous, and it isn't optional. There are fines for not doing it, and in the early days there were volunteers at every bin area checking. How I first learned about it was impossible to miss. There were posters in every building, the bins themselves were replaced overnight, and my neighbours were arguing about it in the lift. I also had a leaflet under my door. Who benefits from it is everyone, eventually. Less rubbish going to landfill, cleaner recycling, and a bit more awareness. It also created a strange new social norm, which is that people now tell each other off about rubbish. How I feel about it is that it works better than I expected. I was sceptical, because rules like this often just annoy people, and it did annoy me for a month. But it's automatic now, and my bin area is cleaner than it was. The lesson is that people will change behaviour if the rule is clear and consistent.","words":200,"p3":[{"q":"What kinds of rules do schools in China have?","a":"Uniforms, no phones in class, arriving on time, and homework deadlines. Most of it is about order rather than learning."},{"q":"Do you think school rules are important?","a":"Yes, because a class of forty without them is not a class. Whether they need to be quite so numerous is a fair question."},{"q":"Are children unhappy with the school rules?","a":"Sometimes, mostly about uniforms and hair. The rules about phones are usually unpopular for about a month and then everyone adjusts."},{"q":"How can parents and teachers help children understand and follow rules?","a":"By explaining the reason, which most adults skip. A rule with a reason can be argued with, and a rule that can be argued with is easier to accept."},{"q":"What are the rules people should obey at work?","a":"Being on time, doing what you said you'd do, and not making your problems someone else's. Beyond that it depends on the job."},{"q":"What is the purpose of punishment?","a":"To protect people, and to make the consequence predictable. Punishment that's unpredictable is just fear, and fear doesn't teach anything."}]},"很久没收到回复的信息":{"mat":"M2 求职邮件","text":"The message that went unanswered for a long time was an email to a school I'd applied to, asking about a part-time position. I sent it in early summer, and I didn't hear anything for about six weeks. It was a fairly ordinary email asking whether the role was still open and whether I could send a CV. I didn't chase it for the first two weeks, because I assumed they were busy. By week three I'd decided they weren't interested. By week five I'd more or less forgotten about it. Why I sent it is that a friend had recommended the school and it fitted exactly what I wanted at the time. The reply did come, eventually, in August, apologising and saying it had gone to the wrong folder. They asked whether I was still interested. How I felt about it was mostly amused, and a bit annoyed. Six weeks is a long time to be invisible. I replied politely and we had a conversation, though nothing came of it. What I took from it is not to take silence personally. Half the time it's an inbox problem, not a judgement.","words":193,"p3":[{"q":"In what situations do people spend a long time responding to others’ messages?","a":"When the message needs an answer they don't have yet, like a quote or a decision. Also when it arrives during work and they mean to reply properly later."},{"q":"In what situations do people not respond to messages at all?","a":"When they've decided not to, mostly. Or when the message doesn't ask anything, so there's nothing to reply to."},{"q":"What would you do if you did not receive a reply after sending out a message?","a":"Wait a day, then send one short follow-up. If that's ignored too, I take it as an answer and stop. Chasing a third time is undignified."},{"q":"Why do some people prefer sending a message instead of making a call?","a":"Because it's less intrusive. A call demands attention now and puts both people on the spot. A message lets the other person answer when they're ready."},{"q":"How do you show your respect in your messages?","a":"By answering in reasonable time, and by not forwarding things that were sent to me privately. Speed is a form of respect."},{"q":"Why do some people feel angry when others don’t reply to their message?","a":"Because it feels like being ignored in public. The read receipt makes it worse, since now they know you saw it and chose not to answer."}]},"长久目标 /抱负":{"mat":"M2 自己的工作室","text":"The long-term goal I've had for the longest is to build my own teaching studio, a small place where I decide who I teach and how. I've had it in some form for about five years, since my third year of teaching, when I realised I wanted to do this for a long time but not inside someone else's institution. How I'll achieve it is slowly and in stages. I've already gone freelance, which was the first step. The next is saving enough to rent a small space and building a group of students who'd follow me there. I've got a rough number in mind and I'm about halfway. Why I set it as a goal is that teaching works better when you control the conditions. In a big institution you get thirty students and a syllabus, whereas in a small studio you can actually see what each one needs. What I like about this goal is that it's measurable and it's in my control. I'm not waiting to be chosen by anyone. I'd like it done in three years. Realistically, four.","words":183,"p3":[{"q":"Why should children have ambitions?","a":"So they have a direction, not so they achieve it. A child with an ambition reads differently, because they're working towards something rather than being moved around."},{"q":"What do you think of people going after high positions?","a":"I don't mind it, as long as they know what the position costs. A lot of people chase a title and find the job underneath is mostly meetings."},{"q":"Why are some young people keen on being fans of superstars?","a":"Because it's a ready-made identity, and being a fan is a way of belonging without having to be good at anything yourself."},{"q":"Is it good for a person to be ambitious?","a":"Within reason. It gets things done and it makes people interesting. The problem is when it becomes the only thing they can talk about."},{"q":"Do you think it is necessary to be ambitious when working in a team in a company?","a":"Less necessary than reliability. A team of ambitious people who don't share information is worse than a team of steady ones."},{"q":"Should parents support their children in pursuing their ambitions?","a":"Support, yes, but not to the point of funding something clearly unrealistic. The useful thing a parent can give is honest feedback plus a place to fail safely."}]},"改变重要想法":{"mat":"M2 对「天赋」的看法","text":"The opinion I changed most substantially was my belief that people are either good at maths or they aren't. I held that view for a long time, including through university. It's a very common view in this country, and it's convenient, because it means the people who are good don't have to explain themselves. I changed it about four years into teaching, mostly through one student. He was hopeless at first, genuinely, and I'd privately written him off. Then I noticed that the things he couldn't do were all one specific thing, dealing with fractions, and once we went back and fixed that, everything above it started to work. Why I changed it is that I saw the same pattern maybe thirty times afterwards. What looks like a lack of talent is usually a specific gap, from years earlier, that nobody went back to. How I felt about it was uncomfortable. I'd been repeating a story that had been told to me, and it had probably done some damage. I'm careful now about what I say to a student who's struggling. It isn't just a fact, it's a forecast.","words":189,"p3":[{"q":"When do most children begin to have their own opinions?","a":"Around seven or eight, I'd say, when they start disagreeing about small things. The opinions are usually borrowed at first, then slowly become their own."},{"q":"Whose opinions are more important to children, their parents' or teachers'?","a":"Parents, in most cases, because they're there every day. Teachers win on specific subjects, but not on how to live."},{"q":"Do children communicate more with teachers or with parents?","a":"With parents, by a wide margin, but not necessarily honestly. Children tell teachers what they think the teacher wants to hear."},{"q":"Who do young people like to share opinions with?","a":"Their friends, mostly. Then social media, which is like a room full of friends who never sleep. Parents come last and usually find out afterwards."}]},"想要颁布的环保法律":{"mat":"独立素材 · 限一次性塑料","text":"The environmental law I'd like my country to introduce is a strict ban on single-use plastics in food delivery and packaging. What the law should be is fairly specific. No plastic cutlery, no plastic bags, no plastic containers for takeaway unless they're reusable or genuinely compostable, and the cost of the alternative has to be carried by the company rather than passed to the customer as an extra fee. There should also be clear labelling so you can tell what's actually recyclable. Why people should follow the law is that the current situation is absurd. One meal can generate four pieces of plastic that will outlive everyone at the table. And the reason people don't change isn't laziness, it's that plastic is the default. Whether it will be popular is the difficult part. Everyone says they care about the environment, but nobody wants their noodles to cost more or arrive in a paper box that leaks. So it would be unpopular for at least a year. How I feel about it is that this is exactly what laws are for. Individual choices can't fix a system built around plastic. I'd accept the soggy boxes.","words":195,"p3":[{"q":"How does technology affect the law?","a":"It makes enforcement possible, cameras and tracking, and it also creates new problems the law didn't anticipate. Data and privacy being the obvious ones."},{"q":"What kinds of rules do schools in China have?","a":"Uniforms, phones, punctuality, and a long list of small safety rules. Every school has its own version, but the shape is the same everywhere."},{"q":"Will there be a law that is universally accepted?","a":"No, I don't think so. Even basic things like not killing people have exceptions in war. What's realistic is agreement on a lot of specific rules, not one universal one."},{"q":"What environmental laws does your country already have?","a":"Waste sorting, limits on plastic bags, factory emissions, and protected areas. The laws exist. Whether they're enforced evenly is the real question."}]},"当地新闻":{"mat":"M6 老楼装电梯","text":"A piece of local news that got everyone talking in my neighbourhood was a plan to install lifts in the old residential buildings. These are six-storey walk-ups built in the eighties, and about half the residents are now elderly. The plan was that the government would pay part of it and the residents the rest. Where I saw it was in the residents' group chat, which is where all local news arrives now, and then in a local news account online. Who was involved was interesting. The people on the ground floor objected, understandably, since they gain nothing and lose light. The people on the fifth and sixth floors were desperate for it. There were public meetings that got quite heated. Why people were interested is that it isn't really about lifts. It's about who pays for ageing, and about how a building full of strangers makes a collective decision. It's still not resolved. Half the buildings on my street have lifts now and half don't. I found it more interesting than national news, honestly. It's the level where you can actually see the argument.","words":187,"p3":[{"q":"Do people read the newspaper where you live?","a":"Less than before, especially younger people. The older generation still buys a paper, and local news also travels through group chats and community notices."},{"q":"Do people prefer local or international news?","a":"Local, mostly, because it's the news people can act on. International news is watched, but it doesn't change what you do on Saturday."},{"q":"Do you think it's important to have a national identity?","a":"Yes, I think so, as long as it doesn't turn into superiority. Knowing where you come from makes it easier to understand people from somewhere else."},{"q":"How can people develop their national identity?","a":"Through language, food, festivals, and stories told at home. School helps, but identity is mostly absorbed rather than taught."}]},"近期改变":{"mat":"独立素材 · 十一点后不看手机","text":"A change I made recently, and I'm slightly proud of, is that I stopped using my phone after eleven at night. What caused it was noticing that I was going to bed at one and getting up exhausted, and that I couldn't remember a single thing I'd read in the last hour of the day. That's a strange way to spend a tenth of your life. What I did was concrete rather than motivational. I bought a cheap alarm clock, charged the phone in the kitchen, and put a book by the bed. The first week I felt like I was missing something. The second week I stopped caring. How I feel about the change is that it was easier than I expected, because it's a rule rather than a decision. I don't have to resist anything at eleven o'clock, the phone simply isn't there. The effect has been bigger than I thought. I sleep better, I read four books in two months, and I'm less irritable in the morning. I still use my phone plenty during the day. I'm not a monk. But that hour belongs to me now.","words":190,"p3":[{"q":"Do you think it is good to change one’s daily routine?","a":"Usually yes, but small changes are better than big ones. Moving one habit, like walking after dinner, tends to stick. Reorganising your whole life on a Monday does not."},{"q":"Do you think it is good to change jobs?","a":"It can be, if the reason is pull rather than push. Leaving because the next thing is better works. Leaving because you hate the current one usually repeats itself."},{"q":"Is it good for people to get a job promotion?","a":"For most people, yes, because it's recognition and money. The trap is that a promotion often means doing less of the work you were good at."},{"q":"Do people often make plans around their regular routines?","a":"Some do, and those are the people who get upset when anything changes. Most people keep a rough routine and plan around the edges of it."},{"q":"Who tend to change their daily routine more, young people or old people?","a":"Young people, clearly. They have fewer commitments and more appetite for disruption. By forty, most people have built a life that only works one way."}]},"印象深刻的课程":{"mat":"独立素材 · 教师培训","text":"The course that impressed me most was a short teacher-training course I took about three years ago, on how students actually learn. It was run by an experienced teacher in a small room with about fifteen of us, over four evenings. The content was mostly about memory and practice, why re-reading doesn't work, why testing yourself does, why spacing things out beats cramming. What we did during the course was unusual. Every session we'd be given a problem and asked to solve it as a class would, then we'd discuss what had helped us and what hadn't. We also had to teach a five-minute segment and be critiqued by the group, which was uncomfortable. Why it impressed me is that it changed my teaching within a week. I stopped explaining things three times and started asking students to recall them instead, and the results were visibly better. What also impressed me is that it was free and nobody was selling anything. It was just a good teacher who wanted to pass something on. I still use the handout. It's four pages and it's worth more than the textbooks I own.","words":193,"p3":[{"q":"What can we learn from history?","a":"That decisions have consequences people didn't foresee, and that most of them were made by someone with incomplete information. That's more useful than dates."},{"q":"What do people need to remember in their daily lives?","a":"Where they put things, and what they promised. Those two cover most of the daily failures. Most people don't need a better memory, they need a system."},{"q":"Can technology help people remember things better? How?","a":"Yes, hugely, because it does the storing so we don't have to. The risk is that we stop rehearsing anything, and then we can't think without it."},{"q":"Which can help people remember things better, words or photos?","a":"Photos, for events. Words, for anything you have to understand. A photo reminds you that something happened, a sentence reminds you why it mattered."},{"q":"Do people like things of memorial significance?","a":"Yes, quite fond of it. Tickets, photographs, a piece of furniture from a grandparent. Objects hold a period of your life in a way that a file doesn't."},{"q":"Why do some people have a better memory than others?","a":"Partly training. Memory champions use techniques anyone can learn. The rest is attention, because most people never really look at what they claim to remember."}]},"想再去一次的城市":{"mat":"M3 西安","text":"The city I'd most like to go back to is Xi'an, which I visited about four years ago for four days. What I did there was a lot of walking. I saw the old city wall and hired a bicycle to go round it, which takes about an hour and a half and is the best thing I did. I ate in the Muslim quarter every evening, mostly things I couldn't identify. I also went to see the terracotta army, which is an hour outside the city and genuinely astonishing, though the crowds are brutal. What it was like is that it felt older than most Chinese cities, and more confident about it. There's a lot of Tang dynasty imagery, the streets are wide, and the food is heavy and wonderful. Why I'd like to visit it again is that four days wasn't enough, and I was there in August, which was a mistake. I'd like to see it in spring, and I'd like to spend a whole day on the wall rather than racing round it. Also, I'd like to eat better. I spent most of my first trip guessing.","words":191,"p3":[{"q":"What should the government do to improve citizens' safety?","a":"Lighting and policing in the obvious places, and reliable public transport at night. Safety is mostly about being able to get home."},{"q":"Are there any changes in your city?","a":"Yes, a lot. New metro lines, whole districts rebuilt, and far more cars. Some of it is better and some of it has lost what made it interesting."},{"q":"Do you think people in the countryside are friendlier than people in the city?","a":"They have more time to talk, so they seem friendlier. Whether they actually are is harder to say. Cities make everyone impatient, not unfriendly."},{"q":"Do you think it is possible that all of the population move to cities?","a":"Not in the foreseeable future, no. Cities need people to grow food and do the work that can't be done in an office."},{"q":"Some people say large cities are suitable for old people. What do you think?","a":"I'd disagree. They're convenient for hospitals and transport, but expensive, noisy, and often lonely. Somewhere in between usually works better."},{"q":"What's the difference between the city and the countryside?","a":"Pace, mostly, and space. The city is fast, dense, and anonymous. The countryside is slow, empty, and everybody knows your business."}]},"一见且想继续了解的人":{"mat":"独立素材 · 火车上的测绘员","text":"The person I met once and would like to know better is a man I sat next to on a train, about a year ago. We were on a two-hour journey and he was reading a book about local architecture, which was unusual enough that I asked him about it. He turned out to be a retired surveyor who now spends his time documenting old buildings that are about to be demolished. We talked for most of the journey. He showed me photographs on his phone, hundreds of them, of doorways and staircases and courtyards, each one labelled with a date and a street. He said he'd been doing it for eleven years and had never shown anyone except his wife. Why I want to know more about him is that he seemed completely uninterested in being impressive. He was doing something useful and slightly pointless, and he knew it was pointless, and he did it anyway. How I feel about him is a bit envious. I'd like to have a project like that. I didn't get his name, which I regret. I only know which district he lives in.","words":191,"p3":[{"q":"What should we not talk about when we meet a person for the first time?","a":"Salary, politics, and anything about appearance. Also, asking whether someone is married within the first two minutes is a classic mistake."},{"q":"What can we do to leave a good impression on people we meet for the first time?","a":"Ask one question and then actually listen to the answer. Most people are so busy preparing their own next sentence that they miss it."},{"q":"What qualities make true friends?","a":"Being reliable, and being pleased to see you. Everything else is optional. Friends you can be silent with are worth more than interesting ones."},{"q":"Is it important to have the same hobbies and interests when making friends?","a":"No, and it can be a trap. Shared hobbies get you in the room. Whether you stay has nothing to do with the hobby."},{"q":"On what occasions do people like to make friends?","a":"At work, at university, and through other people. Almost never at a party, despite what people say."},{"q":"How do people make friends in China?","a":"Through friends and classmates, mostly, and increasingly online. The pattern is the same, an introduction, then a long period of low-stakes contact."}]},"送朋友的礼物":{"mat":"M9 小林","text":"The gift I'd like to buy for a friend is for Lin, the designer I've known for about five years. We met through work, when she designed some materials for a course I was teaching, and we've stayed close since. How I'd choose a gift for her is the easy part, because she's extremely specific. She likes things that are well made and slightly odd, she doesn't like anything with a brand on it, and she has strong opinions about paper. So I'd choose something for her hands rather than something decorative. What I'd buy is a proper set of printmaking tools, a small carving set and a few blocks, because she's been borrowing a studio's equipment for two years and complaining about it. It isn't cheap, but it's the kind of thing she'd never buy for herself. Why I'd choose that is that it's a gift that makes something possible, rather than a gift that sits on a shelf. She'd use it within a week. I'm fairly confident she'd like it. If she doesn't, she'll tell me, which is one reason I like her.","words":185,"p3":[{"q":"Are there any unlucky things that people should not buy as gifts in your country?","a":"Clocks, because the word sounds like the end of something. Umbrellas, for a similar reason. Shoes are also avoided by some people, since they suggest walking away."},{"q":"What do people usually consider when buying gifts?","a":"Whether it's useful, and whether it says you know them. Price comes third. An expensive gift that shows no thought is worse than a cheap one that does."},{"q":"Which do people give on traditional festivals, red envelopes or other kinds of gifts?","a":"Red envelopes for close family, and gifts for everyone else. The envelope is practical, and older relatives prefer it because it can't be the wrong size."},{"q":"Will people feel happy when receiving an expensive gift?","a":"Usually yes, but it can be awkward. If it's far more than they can afford to give back, the pleasure turns into a debt."},{"q":"Is it hard to choose a gift?","a":"Yes, for anyone you know well. It's easy to buy something nice, and hard to buy something they'd have chosen themselves."},{"q":"When do people normally send gifts to others?","a":"Birthdays, weddings, the new year, and when someone has a baby. Also when visiting, which is where the fruit comes in."}]},"公共场所丢物品":{"mat":"独立素材 · 地铁丢手机","text":"The occasion I lost something in public was on the metro, about three years ago, and it was my phone. I was coming home in the evening, standing because it was crowded, and I had it in my coat pocket rather than my bag. When I got off at my stop, it wasn't there. I'm still not sure whether it fell out or was taken. What I did to find it was fairly systematic once I'd stopped panicking. I went back to the station office and asked, I called my own number from a stranger's phone, and I used a friend's laptop to check the find-my-device service. That showed it was still moving, which told me it was on a train. I got it back two days later, from the lost property office at the end of the line. Someone had handed it in. How I felt about it was stupid, mostly, and then lucky. The phone was fine, but the two days without it were oddly peaceful, which was not the lesson I expected. I now keep it in an inside pocket, and I've stopped keeping anything valuable in a coat pocket. That's the whole lesson, really.","words":200,"p3":[{"q":"What do people often feel after losing things?","a":"Annoyed first, then a kind of panic if there's a phone in it. The object matters less than what it unlocks, bank cards, messages, photographs."},{"q":"What can we do to reduce the possibility of losing things?","a":"Have a place for everything, and check the seat before standing up. That's unglamorous and it works better than being careful."},{"q":"What kinds of people may lose things often?","a":"Tired people, distracted people, and anyone carrying too much. In practice, everyone loses things during exam week or a house move."},{"q":"Is it effective to offer rewards to find lost things?","a":"Sometimes, for a wallet or a pet. For a phone, no, because the reward attracts the wrong people. It works better when the item is obviously valuable to one person only."},{"q":"What will people do when losing things?","a":"Retrace their steps, call it, then ask at the desk. Then they usually go home and start cancelling cards, which is the sensible part."},{"q":"What kinds of things do people usually lose?","a":"Phones, wallets, keys, and umbrellas. Then the odd thing that matters enormously, like a notebook or a child's toy."}]},"节约时间的方法 /改变":{"mat":"M2 批量备课","text":"The change that saves me the most time is batching, and specifically never preparing one lesson at a time. What it is, is simple. Instead of planning each class the night before, I now sit down once a week for about two hours and prepare everything for the same subject in one go, using templates I've already got. How I implement it is fairly rigid. Sunday evening, laptop, no phone, and I work through the week's classes in blocks. Each lesson gets a standard structure, a warm-up, three problems, a summary, so I'm not designing anything from scratch, just choosing the content. How difficult it was at the beginning was quite difficult, because it means thinking about Thursday on a Sunday, which I resisted. The first two weeks I did both, the batch and the nightly panic, which felt like double work. How I feel about it now is that it's the single best change I've made to how I work. I've got my evenings back, and the lessons are better, because I'm not preparing them at eleven at night. The real saving isn't the time. It's the attention. Teaching all day and then planning all evening was what was killing me.","words":203,"p3":[{"q":"What can people do to save time?","a":"Decide in advance what not to do. Most people save time on the wrong things, they optimise their email and then spend three hours on short videos."},{"q":"Does technology help people save time? How and why?","a":"Yes and no. It removes the boring parts, then fills the gap with more work. The hours saved don't tend to become free time."},{"q":"Do you think parents should be responsible for teaching children to save time?","a":"Some of it, yes, but school should carry most of it. Parents can show it, teachers can teach it, and children need both."},{"q":"Do people who can manage time well become successful more easily?","a":"More easily, I'd say, but the causality is backwards. People who are already organised get promoted, and then it looks like time management made them successful."},{"q":"Is it necessary for children to learn time management?","a":"Yes, but late, around ten or eleven. Before that it's just being told when to put your shoes on."},{"q":"What can schools do to help students learn time management?","a":"Give them real deadlines and let them feel a small consequence. A project with a date teaches more than a lesson about calendars."}]}}};
